window.JOURNAL_INDEX = [{"id": "paper-2609-18383", "arxiv": "2609.18383", "title": "On the importance of cosmic-ray background in the Atomki anomaly", "authors": "Hicham Benmansour et al.", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The authors construct detailed Geant4 models of the five- and six-arm \\(e^+e^-\\) spectrometers used in the Atomki searches for the proposed \\(17\\) MeV “X17” boson.", "section_text": "**Hicham Benmansour et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.18383](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18383)\n\nThe authors construct detailed Geant4 models of the five- and six-arm \\(e^+e^-\\) spectrometers used in the Atomki searches for the proposed \\(17\\) MeV “X17” boson.\n\nCosmic-ray muons producing two-arm coincidences generate peaks whose locations are controlled by the detectors’ discrete azimuthal geometry:\n\n\\[\n\\theta_{e^+e^-}\\sim140^\\circ\n\\]\n\nin the \\(^{8}\\mathrm{Be}\\) energy window and approximately \\(120^\\circ\\) in the \\(^{4}\\mathrm{He}\\) window. These structures resemble the reported Atomki anomalies not only in angle, but also in their energy-window and pair-asymmetry dependence.\n\nFor the five-arm apparatus, normalization to representative running conditions gives a cosmic-to-internal-pair-conversion ratio above unity in the signal region. The calculation does not prove that cosmic rays explain the anomaly, because the conclusion ultimately depends on the actual beam-off data and event-selection procedure. It does, however, establish cosmic backgrounds as a potentially leading contribution that must be demonstrated experimentally to be under control.\n\n**Assessment:** **important challenge to the X17 interpretation; background study rather than a definitive refutation.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-18383", "editionDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "On the importance of cosmic-ray background in the Atomki anomaly Hicham Benmansour et al. 2609.18383 The authors construct detailed Geant4 models of the five- and six-arm \\(e^+e^-\\) spectrometers used in the Atomki searches for the proposed \\(17\\) MeV “X17” boson. 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 Cosmic rays BSM **Hicham Benmansour et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.18383](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18383)\n\nThe authors construct detailed Geant4 models of the five- and six-arm \\(e^+e^-\\) spectrometers used in the Atomki searches for the proposed \\(17\\) MeV “X17” boson.\n\nCosmic-ray muons producing two-arm coincidences generate peaks whose locations are controlled by the detectors’ discrete azimuthal geometry:\n\n\\[\n\\theta_{e^+e^-}\\sim140^\\circ\n\\]\n\nin the \\(^{8}\\mathrm{Be}\\) energy window and approximately \\(120^\\circ\\) in the \\(^{4}\\mathrm{He}\\) window. These structures resemble the reported Atomki anomalies not only in angle, but also in their energy-window and pair-asymmetry dependence.\n\nFor the five-arm apparatus, normalization to representative running conditions gives a cosmic-to-internal-pair-conversion ratio above unity in the signal region. The calculation does not prove that cosmic rays explain the anomaly, because the conclusion ultimately depends on the actual beam-off data and event-selection procedure. It does, however, establish cosmic backgrounds as a potentially leading contribution that must be demonstrated experimentally to be under control.\n\n**Assessment:** **important challenge to the X17 interpretation; background study rather than a definitive refutation.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-18226", "arxiv": "2609.18226", "title": "Detectability of 100 TeV Gamma Rays from PeV Cosmic Rays Accelerated in the Early Phase of a Supernova Shock Wave", "authors": "Tomotaka Nishikawa, Tsuyoshi Inoue, Alexandre Marcowith", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Ordinary supernova remnants hundreds of years old often appear unable to accelerate particles to the cosmic-ray knee. This work instead examines the first tens of days after a Type II-P explosion, when the shock crosses a dense…", "section_text": "**Tomotaka Nishikawa, Tsuyoshi Inoue, Alexandre Marcowith**  \n[arXiv:2609.18226](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18226)\n\nOrdinary supernova remnants hundreds of years old often appear unable to accelerate particles to the cosmic-ray knee. This work instead examines the first tens of days after a Type II-P explosion, when the shock crosses a dense red-supergiant wind.\n\nUsing cosmic-ray distributions from kinetic-MHD simulations that reach proton energies near \\(3\\) PeV, the authors calculate the time-dependent hadronic gamma-ray signal, including \\(\\gamma\\gamma\\) attenuation on the supernova photospheric radiation field and diffuse cosmic backgrounds.\n\nThe photospheric field suppresses the intrinsic \\(1\\!-\\!100\\) TeV flux by roughly one order of magnitude, but does not erase it. For\n\n\\[\n\\dot M_{\\rm RSG}\\sim10^{-3}\\,M_\\odot\\,{\\rm yr}^{-1},\n\\qquad\nR_{\\rm CSM}\\sim3\\times10^{15}\\ {\\rm cm},\n\\]\n\nthey estimate that a 50-hour CTA observation could detect emission around \\(100\\) TeV from distances up to approximately \\(4\\) Mpc. Reducing the circumstellar extent to \\(2\\times10^{15}\\) cm lowers the horizon to roughly \\(3\\) Mpc.\n\n**Assessment:** **strong, testable prediction for identifying the short-lived PeVatron phase of nearby supernovae.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-18226", "editionDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "Detectability of 100 TeV Gamma Rays from PeV Cosmic Rays Accelerated in the Early Phase of a Supernova Shock Wave Tomotaka Nishikawa, Tsuyoshi Inoue, Alexandre Marcowith 2609.18226 Ordinary supernova remnants hundreds of years old often appear unable to accelerate particles to the cosmic-ray knee. This work instead examines the first tens of days after a Type II-P explosion, when the shock crosses a dense… 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 Cosmic rays Gamma rays Multimessenger **Tomotaka Nishikawa, Tsuyoshi Inoue, Alexandre Marcowith**  \n[arXiv:2609.18226](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18226)\n\nOrdinary supernova remnants hundreds of years old often appear unable to accelerate particles to the cosmic-ray knee. This work instead examines the first tens of days after a Type II-P explosion, when the shock crosses a dense red-supergiant wind.\n\nUsing cosmic-ray distributions from kinetic-MHD simulations that reach proton energies near \\(3\\) PeV, the authors calculate the time-dependent hadronic gamma-ray signal, including \\(\\gamma\\gamma\\) attenuation on the supernova photospheric radiation field and diffuse cosmic backgrounds.\n\nThe photospheric field suppresses the intrinsic \\(1\\!-\\!100\\) TeV flux by roughly one order of magnitude, but does not erase it. For\n\n\\[\n\\dot M_{\\rm RSG}\\sim10^{-3}\\,M_\\odot\\,{\\rm yr}^{-1},\n\\qquad\nR_{\\rm CSM}\\sim3\\times10^{15}\\ {\\rm cm},\n\\]\n\nthey estimate that a 50-hour CTA observation could detect emission around \\(100\\) TeV from distances up to approximately \\(4\\) Mpc. Reducing the circumstellar extent to \\(2\\times10^{15}\\) cm lowers the horizon to roughly \\(3\\) Mpc.\n\n**Assessment:** **strong, testable prediction for identifying the short-lived PeVatron phase of nearby supernovae.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-18247", "arxiv": "2609.18247", "title": "Probing Feebly Interacting Particles with 511 keV Line from Circumstellar Medium of Supernovae", "authors": "Yu Cheng, Chui-Fan Kong, Yen-Hsun Lin, Meng-Ru Wu, Seokhoon Yun", "tags": ["BSM", "Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This paper proposes a new electromagnetic precursor of core-collapse supernovae. Feebly interacting particles produced in the core can escape the star and decay as", "section_text": "**Yu Cheng, Chui-Fan Kong, Yen-Hsun Lin, Meng-Ru Wu, Seokhoon Yun**  \n[arXiv:2609.18247](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18247)\n\nThis paper proposes a new electromagnetic precursor of core-collapse supernovae. Feebly interacting particles produced in the core can escape the star and decay as\n\n\\[\nX\\rightarrow e^+e^- .\n\\]\n\nIf the decay occurs in the circumstellar medium, the positrons slow down and annihilate before the outgoing shock arrives, generating a transient \\(511\\) keV line.\n\nUsing MeV-scale dark photons as a benchmark and circumstellar profiles motivated by SN 2023ixf and SN 2024ggi, the authors find that a Galactic explosion at \\(10\\) kpc could allow COSI—and a future AMEGO-like mission—to probe parameter space beyond existing supernova limits.\n\nThe same deposited energy can heat the circumstellar gas and destroy dust, producing a rapid infrared-to-optical/UV transition. A coincident \\(511\\) keV line and dust-sublimation signature would therefore be substantially harder to mimic than either signal alone.\n\n**Assessment:** **genuinely new multimessenger search channel; forecast contingent on a nearby core-collapse supernova.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-18247", "editionDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "Probing Feebly Interacting Particles with 511 keV Line from Circumstellar Medium of Supernovae Yu Cheng, Chui-Fan Kong, Yen-Hsun Lin, Meng-Ru Wu, Seokhoon Yun 2609.18247 This paper proposes a new electromagnetic precursor of core-collapse supernovae. Feebly interacting particles produced in the core can escape the star and decay as 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 BSM Gamma rays Multimessenger **Yu Cheng, Chui-Fan Kong, Yen-Hsun Lin, Meng-Ru Wu, Seokhoon Yun**  \n[arXiv:2609.18247](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18247)\n\nThis paper proposes a new electromagnetic precursor of core-collapse supernovae. Feebly interacting particles produced in the core can escape the star and decay as\n\n\\[\nX\\rightarrow e^+e^- .\n\\]\n\nIf the decay occurs in the circumstellar medium, the positrons slow down and annihilate before the outgoing shock arrives, generating a transient \\(511\\) keV line.\n\nUsing MeV-scale dark photons as a benchmark and circumstellar profiles motivated by SN 2023ixf and SN 2024ggi, the authors find that a Galactic explosion at \\(10\\) kpc could allow COSI—and a future AMEGO-like mission—to probe parameter space beyond existing supernova limits.\n\nThe same deposited energy can heat the circumstellar gas and destroy dust, producing a rapid infrared-to-optical/UV transition. A coincident \\(511\\) keV line and dust-sublimation signature would therefore be substantially harder to mimic than either signal alone.\n\n**Assessment:** **genuinely new multimessenger search channel; forecast contingent on a nearby core-collapse supernova.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-17705", "arxiv": "2609.17705", "title": "Assessment of Super- and Hyper-Kamiokande Sensitivity to the DSNB", "authors": "Pablo Blanco-Mas, André de Gouvêa, Iván Martínez-Soler", "tags": ["Neutrinos"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The authors reconstruct the current Super-Kamiokande limits on the diffuse supernova neutrino background and forecast ten-year sensitivities for Super-Kamiokande and Hyper-Kamiokande.", "section_text": "**Pablo Blanco-Mas, André de Gouvêa, Iván Martínez-Soler**  \n[arXiv:2609.17705](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17705)\n\nThe authors reconstruct the current Super-Kamiokande limits on the diffuse supernova neutrino background and forecast ten-year sensitivities for Super-Kamiokande and Hyper-Kamiokande.\n\nThe technically important component is their treatment of atmospheric-neutrino backgrounds. Oscillations and propagation modify the flavor and energy distributions entering the DSNB window; approximating these backgrounds too crudely can bias both the discovery significance and the reconstructed DSNB flux.\n\nTheir conclusion is that additional Super-K exposure produces only modest sensitivity gains, whereas Hyper-K has the potential for a statistically significant DSNB detection after approximately ten years.\n\n**Assessment:** **useful quantitative sensitivity study; no new detection, but careful background treatment affects realistic forecasts.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-17705", "editionDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "Assessment of Super- and Hyper-Kamiokande Sensitivity to the DSNB Pablo Blanco-Mas, André de Gouvêa, Iván Martínez-Soler 2609.17705 The authors reconstruct the current Super-Kamiokande limits on the diffuse supernova neutrino background and forecast ten-year sensitivities for Super-Kamiokande and Hyper-Kamiokande. 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 Neutrinos **Pablo Blanco-Mas, André de Gouvêa, Iván Martínez-Soler**  \n[arXiv:2609.17705](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17705)\n\nThe authors reconstruct the current Super-Kamiokande limits on the diffuse supernova neutrino background and forecast ten-year sensitivities for Super-Kamiokande and Hyper-Kamiokande.\n\nThe technically important component is their treatment of atmospheric-neutrino backgrounds. Oscillations and propagation modify the flavor and energy distributions entering the DSNB window; approximating these backgrounds too crudely can bias both the discovery significance and the reconstructed DSNB flux.\n\nTheir conclusion is that additional Super-K exposure produces only modest sensitivity gains, whereas Hyper-K has the potential for a statistically significant DSNB detection after approximately ten years.\n\n**Assessment:** **useful quantitative sensitivity study; no new detection, but careful background treatment affects realistic forecasts.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-18353", "arxiv": "2609.18353", "title": "The unidentified PeV source LHAASO J2108+5157: A microquasar remnant?", "authors": "Josep Martí et al.", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Gamma rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "LHAASO J2108+5157 emits up to hundreds of TeV but has no obvious conventional counterpart. Its compact GeV emission and hard TeV–PeV component are separated by stringent intermediate-energy limits from VERITAS and LST-1.", "section_text": "**Josep Martí et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.18353](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18353)\n\nLHAASO J2108+5157 emits up to hundreds of TeV but has no obvious conventional counterpart. Its compact GeV emission and hard TeV–PeV component are separated by stringent intermediate-energy limits from VERITAS and LST-1.\n\nThe proposed explanation is a past microquasar outburst injecting one population of relativistic protons. Energy-dependent diffusion then separates that population:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{lower-energy protons}&\\rightarrow\n\\text{remain near dense gas}\\rightarrow\\text{GeV emission},\\\\\n\\text{higher-energy protons}&\\rightarrow\n\\text{reach more distant gas}\\rightarrow\\text{TeV--PeV emission}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe same transport model accounts for the compact–extended morphological difference and the absence of a bright present-day radio or X-ray source.\n\n**Assessment:** **coherent new Galactic-PeVatron interpretation, but presently a viable source model rather than a unique identification.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-18353", "editionDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "The unidentified PeV source LHAASO J2108+5157: A microquasar remnant? Josep Martí et al. 2609.18353 LHAASO J2108+5157 emits up to hundreds of TeV but has no obvious conventional counterpart. Its compact GeV emission and hard TeV–PeV component are separated by stringent intermediate-energy limits from VERITAS and LST-1. 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 Cosmic rays Gamma rays **Josep Martí et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.18353](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18353)\n\nLHAASO J2108+5157 emits up to hundreds of TeV but has no obvious conventional counterpart. Its compact GeV emission and hard TeV–PeV component are separated by stringent intermediate-energy limits from VERITAS and LST-1.\n\nThe proposed explanation is a past microquasar outburst injecting one population of relativistic protons. Energy-dependent diffusion then separates that population:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{lower-energy protons}&\\rightarrow\n\\text{remain near dense gas}\\rightarrow\\text{GeV emission},\\\\\n\\text{higher-energy protons}&\\rightarrow\n\\text{reach more distant gas}\\rightarrow\\text{TeV--PeV emission}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe same transport model accounts for the compact–extended morphological difference and the absence of a bright present-day radio or X-ray source.\n\n**Assessment:** **coherent new Galactic-PeVatron interpretation, but presently a viable source model rather than a unique identification.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-17665", "arxiv": "2609.17665", "title": "Unifying spatial correlation with hierarchical population inference to constrain the AGN channel for binary black hole mergers", "authors": "Lucas Pouw, Lorenzo Speri, Niccolò Veronesi, Elena Maria Rossi", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Previous tests of binary-black-hole formation in AGN disks generally used either correlations between GW localizations and AGN catalogues or the inferred masses and spins of the binaries. This paper combines both sources of information…", "section_text": "**Lucas Pouw, Lorenzo Speri, Niccolò Veronesi, Elena Maria Rossi**  \n[arXiv:2609.17665](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17665)\n\nPrevious tests of binary-black-hole formation in AGN disks generally used either correlations between GW localizations and AGN catalogues or the inferred masses and spins of the binaries. This paper combines both sources of information in one hierarchical Bayesian likelihood.\n\nApplied to GWTC-5.0 and the Quaia AGN catalogue, the analysis does not exclude an AGN contribution. For unobscured AGN above three luminosity thresholds,\n\n\\[\nL_{\\rm bol}\\geq\n\\{10^{44.5},10^{45.5},10^{46.5}\\}\\ {\\rm erg\\,s^{-1}},\n\\]\n\nthe corresponding 95% credible upper limits on the AGN-hosted merger fraction are approximately\n\n\\[\nf_{\\rm AGN}<\\{0.52,0.41,0.61\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe current bounds are therefore moderate, but the joint framework should become considerably more informative as GW localization, population statistics and AGN catalogues improve.\n\n**Assessment:** **significant methodological advance in GW–AGN population inference; current data do not yet isolate the AGN channel.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-17665", "editionDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "Unifying spatial correlation with hierarchical population inference to constrain the AGN channel for binary black hole mergers Lucas Pouw, Lorenzo Speri, Niccolò Veronesi, Elena Maria Rossi 2609.17665 Previous tests of binary-black-hole formation in AGN disks generally used either correlations between GW localizations and AGN catalogues or the inferred masses and spins of the binaries. This paper combines both sources of information… 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 Multimessenger **Lucas Pouw, Lorenzo Speri, Niccolò Veronesi, Elena Maria Rossi**  \n[arXiv:2609.17665](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17665)\n\nPrevious tests of binary-black-hole formation in AGN disks generally used either correlations between GW localizations and AGN catalogues or the inferred masses and spins of the binaries. This paper combines both sources of information in one hierarchical Bayesian likelihood.\n\nApplied to GWTC-5.0 and the Quaia AGN catalogue, the analysis does not exclude an AGN contribution. For unobscured AGN above three luminosity thresholds,\n\n\\[\nL_{\\rm bol}\\geq\n\\{10^{44.5},10^{45.5},10^{46.5}\\}\\ {\\rm erg\\,s^{-1}},\n\\]\n\nthe corresponding 95% credible upper limits on the AGN-hosted merger fraction are approximately\n\n\\[\nf_{\\rm AGN}<\\{0.52,0.41,0.61\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe current bounds are therefore moderate, but the joint framework should become considerably more informative as GW localization, population statistics and AGN catalogues improve.\n\n**Assessment:** **significant methodological advance in GW–AGN population inference; current data do not yet isolate the AGN channel.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-17661", "arxiv": "2609.17661", "title": "1D PIC Simulations of Resonant Scattering-Driven Pair Cascades in Magnetar Magnetospheres", "authors": "Jens F. Mahlmann et al.", "tags": ["Gamma rays"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "These particle-in-cell simulations model resonant inverse-Compton scattering, pair creation, particle acceleration and radiative drag self-consistently along twisted magnetar field lines.", "section_text": "**Jens F. Mahlmann et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.17661](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17661)\n\nThese particle-in-cell simulations model resonant inverse-Compton scattering, pair creation, particle acceleration and radiative drag self-consistently along twisted magnetar field lines.\n\nDespite strong drag opposing both electron and positron flows, the circuit reorganizes into a single accelerating gap near the stellar surface in one hemisphere. The resulting outflow becomes heavily pair-loaded, while ions extracted from the surface help transfer momentum across the equatorial region through streaming instabilities.\n\nA notable prediction is that confinement of the gap to one hemisphere should produce asymmetric hard-X-ray emission that may be visible in phase-resolved spectra.\n\n**Assessment:** **valuable kinetic result connecting pair-cascade microphysics to an observable magnetar asymmetry; limited for now to one-dimensional field-line dynamics.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-17661", "editionDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "1D PIC Simulations of Resonant Scattering-Driven Pair Cascades in Magnetar Magnetospheres Jens F. Mahlmann et al. 2609.17661 These particle-in-cell simulations model resonant inverse-Compton scattering, pair creation, particle acceleration and radiative drag self-consistently along twisted magnetar field lines. 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 Gamma rays **Jens F. Mahlmann et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.17661](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17661)\n\nThese particle-in-cell simulations model resonant inverse-Compton scattering, pair creation, particle acceleration and radiative drag self-consistently along twisted magnetar field lines.\n\nDespite strong drag opposing both electron and positron flows, the circuit reorganizes into a single accelerating gap near the stellar surface in one hemisphere. The resulting outflow becomes heavily pair-loaded, while ions extracted from the surface help transfer momentum across the equatorial region through streaming instabilities.\n\nA notable prediction is that confinement of the gap to one hemisphere should produce asymmetric hard-X-ray emission that may be visible in phase-resolved spectra.\n\n**Assessment:** **valuable kinetic result connecting pair-cascade microphysics to an observable magnetar asymmetry; limited for now to one-dimensional field-line dynamics.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-17672", "arxiv": "2609.17672", "title": "WIMPs with Enhanced Annihilation from a Feebly Interacting Unstable Partner", "authors": "Chance Hoskinson, Pearl Sandick, Barmak Shams Es Haghi", "tags": ["Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The authors extend thermal WIMP cosmology by adding a heavier, feebly interacting state populated through inverse decays while WIMPs remain in equilibrium.", "section_text": "**Chance Hoskinson, Pearl Sandick, Barmak Shams Es Haghi**  \n[arXiv:2609.17672](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17672)\n\nThe authors extend thermal WIMP cosmology by adding a heavier, feebly interacting state populated through inverse decays while WIMPs remain in equilibrium.\n\nIf the partner decays after WIMP freeze-out, it injects a nonthermal WIMP population. Obtaining the observed relic density then requires re-annihilation and an annihilation cross section potentially up to\n\n\\[\n\\langle\\sigma v\\rangle\\sim10^3\n\\langle\\sigma v\\rangle_{\\rm canonical}.\n\\]\n\nThis creates a relic-density mechanism that remains WIMP-like but predicts much stronger present-day indirect-detection signals. A light-higgsino realization is exhibited in the NMSSM.\n\n**Assessment:** **interesting modification of standard freeze-out with clear indirect-detection consequences; primarily a theoretical framework rather than a new constraint.**\n\n---\n\nNo genuinely new experimental **UHECR composition/anisotropy measurement** or precision **neutrino-oscillation result** appears in today’s batch. The paper on lensing-induced neutrino decoherence, [arXiv:2609.18202](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18202), presents a novel idea, but its observational utility depends critically on whether the relevant astrophysical neutrino wave packets retain sufficient coherence before encountering the proposed stochastic lensing structure.", "path": "2026/09/17/index.html#paper-2609-17672", "editionDate": "17 September 2026", "searchText": "WIMPs with Enhanced Annihilation from a Feebly Interacting Unstable Partner Chance Hoskinson, Pearl Sandick, Barmak Shams Es Haghi 2609.17672 The authors extend thermal WIMP cosmology by adding a heavier, feebly interacting state populated through inverse decays while WIMPs remain in equilibrium. 2026-09-17 17 September 2026 Dark matter BSM **Chance Hoskinson, Pearl Sandick, Barmak Shams Es Haghi**  \n[arXiv:2609.17672](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17672)\n\nThe authors extend thermal WIMP cosmology by adding a heavier, feebly interacting state populated through inverse decays while WIMPs remain in equilibrium.\n\nIf the partner decays after WIMP freeze-out, it injects a nonthermal WIMP population. Obtaining the observed relic density then requires re-annihilation and an annihilation cross section potentially up to\n\n\\[\n\\langle\\sigma v\\rangle\\sim10^3\n\\langle\\sigma v\\rangle_{\\rm canonical}.\n\\]\n\nThis creates a relic-density mechanism that remains WIMP-like but predicts much stronger present-day indirect-detection signals. A light-higgsino realization is exhibited in the NMSSM.\n\n**Assessment:** **interesting modification of standard freeze-out with clear indirect-detection consequences; primarily a theoretical framework rather than a new constraint.**\n\n---\n\nNo genuinely new experimental **UHECR composition/anisotropy measurement** or precision **neutrino-oscillation result** appears in today’s batch. The paper on lensing-induced neutrino decoherence, [arXiv:2609.18202](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18202), presents a novel idea, but its observational utility depends critically on whether the relevant astrophysical neutrino wave packets retain sufficient coherence before encountering the proposed stochastic lensing structure."}, {"id": "paper-2609-17275", "arxiv": "2609.17275", "title": "A fast radio burst from a non-targeted low-frequency survey", "authors": "Cristian Di Pietrantonio et al.", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Using an imaging-based search of Murchison Widefield Array observations, the authors report the first blind—or non-targeted—FRB detection below 300 MHz. The only previously confirmed FRB at such frequencies came from targeted monitoring…", "section_text": "**Cristian Di Pietrantonio et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.17275](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17275)\n\nUsing an imaging-based search of Murchison Widefield Array observations, the authors report the **first blind—or non-targeted—FRB detection below 300 MHz**. The only previously confirmed FRB at such frequencies came from targeted monitoring of the already-known repeater FRB 20180916B.\n\nThis matters physically because plasma scattering and free–free absorption should become much stronger toward low frequencies. The inferred event rate is nevertheless compatible with rates measured above 400 MHz, disfavoring explanations in which severe low-frequency suppression is generic across the FRB population. It also demonstrates that wide-field imaging searches can handle the tens-of-seconds dispersion delays that make conventional low-frequency transient searches computationally difficult.\n\n**Assessment:** **genuinely new observational milestone**, although a population-level conclusion will require more than one blind detection.\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-17275", "editionDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "A fast radio burst from a non-targeted low-frequency survey Cristian Di Pietrantonio et al. 2609.17275 Using an imaging-based search of Murchison Widefield Array observations, the authors report the first blind—or non-targeted—FRB detection below 300 MHz. The only previously confirmed FRB at such frequencies came from targeted monitoring… 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Multimessenger **Cristian Di Pietrantonio et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.17275](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17275)\n\nUsing an imaging-based search of Murchison Widefield Array observations, the authors report the **first blind—or non-targeted—FRB detection below 300 MHz**. The only previously confirmed FRB at such frequencies came from targeted monitoring of the already-known repeater FRB 20180916B.\n\nThis matters physically because plasma scattering and free–free absorption should become much stronger toward low frequencies. The inferred event rate is nevertheless compatible with rates measured above 400 MHz, disfavoring explanations in which severe low-frequency suppression is generic across the FRB population. It also demonstrates that wide-field imaging searches can handle the tens-of-seconds dispersion delays that make conventional low-frequency transient searches computationally difficult.\n\n**Assessment:** **genuinely new observational milestone**, although a population-level conclusion will require more than one blind detection.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-17315", "arxiv": "2609.17315", "title": "Caught by its own light: Wimpzillas, the LHAASO knee and the diffuse γ-ray sky", "authors": "D. Akl, A. J. Iovino, D. Kantzas, A. V. Macciò", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "Gamma rays", "Dark matter"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The authors test whether decays of superheavy dark matter in the Galactic halo could explain both the proton hardening at approximately", "section_text": "**D. Akl, A. J. Iovino, D. Kantzas, A. V. Macciò**  \n[arXiv:2609.17315](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17315)\n\nThe authors test whether decays of superheavy dark matter in the Galactic halo could explain both the proton hardening at approximately\n\n\\[\nE\\simeq0.34~{\\rm PeV}\n\\]\n\nand the knee near\n\n\\[\nE\\simeq3.3~{\\rm PeV}\n\\]\n\nreported by LHAASO. A decaying relic with\n\n\\[\nm_\\chi\\simeq5\\times10^7~{\\rm GeV}\n\\]\n\nprovides a good fit to the proton spectrum across all three hadronic-interaction-model reconstructions considered.\n\nThe same hadronic cascade inevitably produces photons, however. Those photons exceed the measured cosmic-ray photon fraction by roughly **two orders of magnitude** and the LHAASO diffuse Galactic gamma-ray emission by about **one order of magnitude**.\n\nThe result is a particularly clean example of a multimessenger consistency test:\n\n\\[\n\\text{fit to CR nucleons}\n\\quad\\not\\Rightarrow\\quad\n\\text{viable model once }\\gamma\\text{-rays are included}.\n\\]\n\n**Assessment:** **strong new exclusion result**; the proposed Wimpzilla explanation of the LHAASO proton features is effectively ruled out in this channel.\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-17315", "editionDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "Caught by its own light: Wimpzillas, the LHAASO knee and the diffuse γ-ray sky D. Akl, A. J. Iovino, D. Kantzas, A. V. Macciò 2609.17315 The authors test whether decays of superheavy dark matter in the Galactic halo could explain both the proton hardening at approximately 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Cosmic rays Gamma rays Dark matter **D. Akl, A. J. Iovino, D. Kantzas, A. V. Macciò**  \n[arXiv:2609.17315](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17315)\n\nThe authors test whether decays of superheavy dark matter in the Galactic halo could explain both the proton hardening at approximately\n\n\\[\nE\\simeq0.34~{\\rm PeV}\n\\]\n\nand the knee near\n\n\\[\nE\\simeq3.3~{\\rm PeV}\n\\]\n\nreported by LHAASO. A decaying relic with\n\n\\[\nm_\\chi\\simeq5\\times10^7~{\\rm GeV}\n\\]\n\nprovides a good fit to the proton spectrum across all three hadronic-interaction-model reconstructions considered.\n\nThe same hadronic cascade inevitably produces photons, however. Those photons exceed the measured cosmic-ray photon fraction by roughly **two orders of magnitude** and the LHAASO diffuse Galactic gamma-ray emission by about **one order of magnitude**.\n\nThe result is a particularly clean example of a multimessenger consistency test:\n\n\\[\n\\text{fit to CR nucleons}\n\\quad\\not\\Rightarrow\\quad\n\\text{viable model once }\\gamma\\text{-rays are included}.\n\\]\n\n**Assessment:** **strong new exclusion result**; the proposed Wimpzilla explanation of the LHAASO proton features is effectively ruled out in this channel.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-16425", "arxiv": "2609.16425", "title": "Right Energy, Wrong Profile: Why the 43 GeV Cluster Line Is Unlikely to Be Dark Matter", "authors": "Stefano Profumo", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Dark matter"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This reanalysis revisits the reported narrow Fermi-LAT feature around 43 GeV from the Virgo, Fornax and Ophiuchus clusters.", "section_text": "**Stefano Profumo**  \n[arXiv:2609.16425](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16425)\n\nThis reanalysis revisits the reported narrow Fermi-LAT feature around 43 GeV from the Virgo, Fornax and Ophiuchus clusters.\n\nThe excess remains present under several instrumental and data-splitting checks, but its inferred morphology is unexpectedly broad: uniform surface brightness or strongly substructure-enhanced emission is preferred over a smooth NFW annihilation template or central point source. Accounting for the restricted energy and morphology scan gives a conditional global significance of only about\n\n\\[\n2.7\\sigma,\n\\]\n\nwith the maximum near \\(44.5\\) GeV. Because the three-cluster sample had already been selected, this is not an unconditional discovery significance.\n\nA dark-matter interpretation would require an extreme subhalo boost and a large \\(\\gamma\\gamma\\) branching fraction, leading to an annihilation cross section already in tension with stronger Galactic-halo line searches. The favored explanation is therefore a statistical fluctuation, possibly assisted by diffuse-background or unresolved-source mismodelling.\n\n**Assessment:** **substantive independent scrutiny of an anomaly**; not evidence for a gamma-ray line or dark matter.\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-16425", "editionDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "Right Energy, Wrong Profile: Why the 43 GeV Cluster Line Is Unlikely to Be Dark Matter Stefano Profumo 2609.16425 This reanalysis revisits the reported narrow Fermi-LAT feature around 43 GeV from the Virgo, Fornax and Ophiuchus clusters. 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Gamma rays Dark matter **Stefano Profumo**  \n[arXiv:2609.16425](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16425)\n\nThis reanalysis revisits the reported narrow Fermi-LAT feature around 43 GeV from the Virgo, Fornax and Ophiuchus clusters.\n\nThe excess remains present under several instrumental and data-splitting checks, but its inferred morphology is unexpectedly broad: uniform surface brightness or strongly substructure-enhanced emission is preferred over a smooth NFW annihilation template or central point source. Accounting for the restricted energy and morphology scan gives a conditional global significance of only about\n\n\\[\n2.7\\sigma,\n\\]\n\nwith the maximum near \\(44.5\\) GeV. Because the three-cluster sample had already been selected, this is not an unconditional discovery significance.\n\nA dark-matter interpretation would require an extreme subhalo boost and a large \\(\\gamma\\gamma\\) branching fraction, leading to an annihilation cross section already in tension with stronger Galactic-halo line searches. The favored explanation is therefore a statistical fluctuation, possibly assisted by diffuse-background or unresolved-source mismodelling.\n\n**Assessment:** **substantive independent scrutiny of an anomaly**; not evidence for a gamma-ray line or dark matter.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-16529", "arxiv": "2609.16529", "title": "Dark Matter Inelastic Scattering with Nuclei for Direct Detection", "authors": "Shao-Feng Ge, Oleg Titov, Yakun Wang", "tags": ["Dark matter"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The paper calculates elastic and inelastic WIMP response functions for 129Xe and 131Xe using relativistic configuration-interaction density-functional theory rather than the nuclear shell model normally adopted in xenon analyses.", "section_text": "**Shao-Feng Ge, Oleg Titov, Yakun Wang**  \n[arXiv:2609.16529](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16529)\n\nThe paper calculates elastic and inelastic WIMP response functions for \\(^{129}\\mathrm{Xe}\\) and \\(^{131}\\mathrm{Xe}\\) using relativistic configuration-interaction density-functional theory rather than the nuclear shell model normally adopted in xenon analyses.\n\nAfter performing the multipole expansion and imposing angular-momentum, parity and time-reversal selection rules, the authors find that nuclear excitation cannot always be treated as a small correction. Depending on the effective DM operator:\n\n- the inelastic rate can be comparable to the elastic rate;\n- for some operators it can dominate by as much as three orders of magnitude;\n- transitions to higher excited states can be as important as the first excited state.\n\nThe resulting response functions differ significantly from shell-model calculations in some channels, potentially changing the interpretation of xenon direct-detection limits and high-energy recoil events.\n\n**Assessment:** **important nuclear-theory input for DM searches**, rather than a new experimental signal.\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-16529", "editionDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "Dark Matter Inelastic Scattering with Nuclei for Direct Detection Shao-Feng Ge, Oleg Titov, Yakun Wang 2609.16529 The paper calculates elastic and inelastic WIMP response functions for 129Xe and 131Xe using relativistic configuration-interaction density-functional theory rather than the nuclear shell model normally adopted in xenon analyses. 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Dark matter **Shao-Feng Ge, Oleg Titov, Yakun Wang**  \n[arXiv:2609.16529](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16529)\n\nThe paper calculates elastic and inelastic WIMP response functions for \\(^{129}\\mathrm{Xe}\\) and \\(^{131}\\mathrm{Xe}\\) using relativistic configuration-interaction density-functional theory rather than the nuclear shell model normally adopted in xenon analyses.\n\nAfter performing the multipole expansion and imposing angular-momentum, parity and time-reversal selection rules, the authors find that nuclear excitation cannot always be treated as a small correction. Depending on the effective DM operator:\n\n- the inelastic rate can be comparable to the elastic rate;\n- for some operators it can dominate by as much as three orders of magnitude;\n- transitions to higher excited states can be as important as the first excited state.\n\nThe resulting response functions differ significantly from shell-model calculations in some channels, potentially changing the interpretation of xenon direct-detection limits and high-energy recoil events.\n\n**Assessment:** **important nuclear-theory input for DM searches**, rather than a new experimental signal.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-16139", "arxiv": "2609.16139", "title": "Multiple-Mediator-Affected Ultra-High-Energy Neutrino Attenuation: Hints for 5D U(1)Lμ−Lτ in IceCube", "authors": "Atri Bhattacharya, Ayushi Kaushik, Kenji Nishiwaki", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The authors attempt to reproduce the softening of the IceCube spectrum above tens of TeV through scattering of astrophysical neutrinos on the cosmic neutrino background.", "section_text": "**Atri Bhattacharya, Ayushi Kaushik, Kenji Nishiwaki**  \n[arXiv:2609.16139](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16139)\n\nThe authors attempt to reproduce the softening of the IceCube spectrum above tens of TeV through scattering of astrophysical neutrinos on the cosmic neutrino background.\n\nInstead of one \\(L_\\mu-L_\\tau\\) mediator, a compact fifth dimension generates a Kaluza–Klein tower. This produces multiple nearby \\(s\\)-channel resonances, supplemented by \\(t\\)- and \\(u\\)-channel scattering. Repeated interactions can consequently create broad, energy-dependent attenuation rather than the isolated absorption dip typical of a single mediator.\n\nThe mechanism can soften an injected single power law, but its interpretation is highly model-dependent: it depends on the compactification scale, coupling, mediator spectrum, neutrino masses and assumed unattenuated source population. “Hints” in the title should therefore not be read as statistical evidence for an extra dimension.\n\n**Assessment:** **interesting but speculative neutrino-BSM phenomenology**.\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-16139", "editionDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "Multiple-Mediator-Affected Ultra-High-Energy Neutrino Attenuation: Hints for 5D U(1)Lμ−Lτ in IceCube Atri Bhattacharya, Ayushi Kaushik, Kenji Nishiwaki 2609.16139 The authors attempt to reproduce the softening of the IceCube spectrum above tens of TeV through scattering of astrophysical neutrinos on the cosmic neutrino background. 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Neutrinos BSM **Atri Bhattacharya, Ayushi Kaushik, Kenji Nishiwaki**  \n[arXiv:2609.16139](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16139)\n\nThe authors attempt to reproduce the softening of the IceCube spectrum above tens of TeV through scattering of astrophysical neutrinos on the cosmic neutrino background.\n\nInstead of one \\(L_\\mu-L_\\tau\\) mediator, a compact fifth dimension generates a Kaluza–Klein tower. This produces multiple nearby \\(s\\)-channel resonances, supplemented by \\(t\\)- and \\(u\\)-channel scattering. Repeated interactions can consequently create broad, energy-dependent attenuation rather than the isolated absorption dip typical of a single mediator.\n\nThe mechanism can soften an injected single power law, but its interpretation is highly model-dependent: it depends on the compactification scale, coupling, mediator spectrum, neutrino masses and assumed unattenuated source population. “Hints” in the title should therefore not be read as statistical evidence for an extra dimension.\n\n**Assessment:** **interesting but speculative neutrino-BSM phenomenology**.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-16547", "arxiv": "2609.16547", "title": "Probing multi-state dark matter via optical absorption lines in DESI spectra", "authors": "Anoma Ganguly, Shadab Alam", "tags": ["Dark matter"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "For two-state dark matter with a magnetic-dipole transition, halo DM can resonantly absorb photons with", "section_text": "**Anoma Ganguly, Shadab Alam**  \n[arXiv:2609.16547](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16547)\n\nFor two-state dark matter with a magnetic-dipole transition, halo DM can resonantly absorb photons with\n\n\\[\nE_\\gamma=\\Delta E,\n\\]\n\nproducing a narrow line in the spectra of background galaxies or quasars.\n\nThe authors stack **145,087 DESI Early Data Release spectra** whose sightlines pass through foreground GAMA galaxy groups. No absorption line is found, and they derive \\(2\\sigma\\) limits on the transition cross section.\n\nThe strongest sensitivity occurs for splittings around \\(3\\!-\\!5\\) eV and DM masses of a few MeV, including parameter space poorly covered by direct detection and conventional BBN/CMB limits. The full DESI sample could improve the sensitivity by more than an order of magnitude.\n\n**Assessment:** **genuinely new observational search technique and first constraint**, currently statistics-limited.\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-16547", "editionDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "Probing multi-state dark matter via optical absorption lines in DESI spectra Anoma Ganguly, Shadab Alam 2609.16547 For two-state dark matter with a magnetic-dipole transition, halo DM can resonantly absorb photons with 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Dark matter **Anoma Ganguly, Shadab Alam**  \n[arXiv:2609.16547](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16547)\n\nFor two-state dark matter with a magnetic-dipole transition, halo DM can resonantly absorb photons with\n\n\\[\nE_\\gamma=\\Delta E,\n\\]\n\nproducing a narrow line in the spectra of background galaxies or quasars.\n\nThe authors stack **145,087 DESI Early Data Release spectra** whose sightlines pass through foreground GAMA galaxy groups. No absorption line is found, and they derive \\(2\\sigma\\) limits on the transition cross section.\n\nThe strongest sensitivity occurs for splittings around \\(3\\!-\\!5\\) eV and DM masses of a few MeV, including parameter space poorly covered by direct detection and conventional BBN/CMB limits. The full DESI sample could improve the sensitivity by more than an order of magnitude.\n\n**Assessment:** **genuinely new observational search technique and first constraint**, currently statistics-limited.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-16826", "arxiv": "2609.16826", "title": "Testing a Pre-Supernova Contribution to 44Ti in Cassiopeia A", "authors": "Toshiki Sato et al.", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The usual interpretation treats the 44Ti mapped by NuSTAR in Cas A as a tracer of asymmetric explosive burning. This paper tests whether a significant fraction could instead have been synthesized before core collapse in an oxygen–carbon…", "section_text": "**Toshiki Sato et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.16826](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16826)\n\nThe usual interpretation treats the \\(^{44}\\mathrm{Ti}\\) mapped by NuSTAR in Cas A as a tracer of asymmetric explosive burning. This paper tests whether a significant fraction could instead have been synthesized before core collapse in an oxygen–carbon shell merger.\n\nThe low- and intermediate-velocity \\(^{44}\\mathrm{Ti}\\) components correlate more strongly with oxygen-layer tracers than with shocked iron-rich material. If that association is pre-supernova in origin, it could account for as much as\n\n\\[\nM_{^{44}\\mathrm{Ti}}\\sim7\\times10^{-5}M_\\odot,\n\\]\n\nroughly half the observed inventory. Producing this amount requires stronger ingestion and convection than standard one-dimensional stellar-evolution models predict. XRISM measurements of stable Fe-group abundance ratios provide an independent restriction on how much \\(^{44}\\mathrm{Ti}\\) can come from explosive burning.\n\n**Assessment:** **interesting reinterpretation of Cas A nucleosynthesis**, with significant stellar-modelling dependence.\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-16826", "editionDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "Testing a Pre-Supernova Contribution to 44Ti in Cassiopeia A Toshiki Sato et al. 2609.16826 The usual interpretation treats the 44Ti mapped by NuSTAR in Cas A as a tracer of asymmetric explosive burning. This paper tests whether a significant fraction could instead have been synthesized before core collapse in an oxygen–carbon… 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Multimessenger **Toshiki Sato et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.16826](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.16826)\n\nThe usual interpretation treats the \\(^{44}\\mathrm{Ti}\\) mapped by NuSTAR in Cas A as a tracer of asymmetric explosive burning. This paper tests whether a significant fraction could instead have been synthesized before core collapse in an oxygen–carbon shell merger.\n\nThe low- and intermediate-velocity \\(^{44}\\mathrm{Ti}\\) components correlate more strongly with oxygen-layer tracers than with shocked iron-rich material. If that association is pre-supernova in origin, it could account for as much as\n\n\\[\nM_{^{44}\\mathrm{Ti}}\\sim7\\times10^{-5}M_\\odot,\n\\]\n\nroughly half the observed inventory. Producing this amount requires stronger ingestion and convection than standard one-dimensional stellar-evolution models predict. XRISM measurements of stable Fe-group abundance ratios provide an independent restriction on how much \\(^{44}\\mathrm{Ti}\\) can come from explosive burning.\n\n**Assessment:** **interesting reinterpretation of Cas A nucleosynthesis**, with significant stellar-modelling dependence.\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-17507", "arxiv": "2609.17507", "title": "Constraints on the central engine of merger-driven long gamma-ray bursts", "authors": "Muskan Yadav et al.", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The paper examines the emerging class of long-duration GRBs apparently produced by compact-object mergers rather than massive-star collapse.", "section_text": "**Muskan Yadav et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.17507](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17507)\n\nThe paper examines the emerging class of long-duration GRBs apparently produced by compact-object mergers rather than massive-star collapse.\n\nATCA observations of seven nearby candidates at \\(2.1\\) GHz, taken \\(520\\!-\\!6900\\) days after the bursts, yield no late-time counterparts, with \\(3\\sigma\\) limits of \\(33\\!-\\!72~\\mu{\\rm Jy}\\). These limits do not yet exclude energetic magnetar-driven ejecta when the circumburst density is low.\n\nCombining the radio nondetections with fallback-powered prompt-emission modelling sharply restricts the allowed magnetar spin, magnetic field and jet baryon loading. A magnetar engine remains possible, but only in a comparatively narrow region of parameter space.\n\n**Assessment:** **useful multiwavelength constraint**, not yet a decisive rejection of magnetar remnants.\n\n---\n\nThere is **no sufficiently important new neutrino-oscillation result or new UHECR anisotropy/composition measurement** in today’s batch. The main neutrino item is the extra-dimensional attenuation model above; it is theoretical and exploratory.", "path": "2026/09/16/index.html#paper-2609-17507", "editionDate": "16 September 2026", "searchText": "Constraints on the central engine of merger-driven long gamma-ray bursts Muskan Yadav et al. 2609.17507 The paper examines the emerging class of long-duration GRBs apparently produced by compact-object mergers rather than massive-star collapse. 2026-09-16 16 September 2026 Gamma rays Multimessenger **Muskan Yadav et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.17507](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.17507)\n\nThe paper examines the emerging class of long-duration GRBs apparently produced by compact-object mergers rather than massive-star collapse.\n\nATCA observations of seven nearby candidates at \\(2.1\\) GHz, taken \\(520\\!-\\!6900\\) days after the bursts, yield no late-time counterparts, with \\(3\\sigma\\) limits of \\(33\\!-\\!72~\\mu{\\rm Jy}\\). These limits do not yet exclude energetic magnetar-driven ejecta when the circumburst density is low.\n\nCombining the radio nondetections with fallback-powered prompt-emission modelling sharply restricts the allowed magnetar spin, magnetic field and jet baryon loading. A magnetar engine remains possible, but only in a comparatively narrow region of parameter space.\n\n**Assessment:** **useful multiwavelength constraint**, not yet a decisive rejection of magnetar remnants.\n\n---\n\nThere is **no sufficiently important new neutrino-oscillation result or new UHECR anisotropy/composition measurement** in today’s batch. The main neutrino item is the extra-dimensional attenuation model above; it is theoretical and exploratory."}, {"id": "paper-2609-14955", "arxiv": "2609.14955", "title": "Measurement of Cosmic-Ray Density in the Spiral Arms toward Galactic Anticenter", "authors": "Jia-hao Liu, Bing Liu, Rui-zhi Yang", "tags": ["Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Using almost 17 years of Fermi-LAT data, they isolate diffuse gamma-ray emission associated with the Local, Perseus and Outer spiral arms over 105∘<l<145∘. All three components show a clear pion bump, so the inferred emissivity is…", "section_text": "**Jia-hao Liu, Bing Liu, Rui-zhi Yang**\n[arXiv:2609.14955](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.14955)\n\nUsing almost **17 years of Fermi-LAT data**, they isolate diffuse gamma-ray emission associated with the Local, Perseus and Outer spiral arms over \\(105^\\circ<l<145^\\circ\\). All three components show a clear **pion bump**, so the inferred emissivity is directly tracing the underlying CR-proton population rather than relying on a purely phenomenological gamma-ray spectrum.\n\nThey obtain proton spectral indices approximately\n\\[\n\\Gamma_p\\simeq\n2.75\\quad{\\rm Local},\\qquad\n2.55\\quad{\\rm Perseus},\\qquad\n2.80\\quad{\\rm Outer},\n\\]\nwith CR energy densities above \\(10\\) GeV of order\n\\[\nu_{\\rm CR}\\sim0.2~{\\rm eV\\,cm^{-3}}\n\\]\nin all three arms. The important tension is that the outer-Galaxy CR population comes out **denser and harder than standard propagation models usually predict** as Galactocentric radius increases. Possible explanations include a larger CR halo, slower radial decline of the magnetic field, or local acceleration—especially in the Perseus arm.\n\n**Significance:** it is a clean observational handle on the radial dependence of Galactic CR transport, with obvious implications for diffuse gamma rays and Galactic-neutrino modelling.\n\n**Classification:** **genuinely useful observational CR result.**", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-14955", "editionDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "Measurement of Cosmic-Ray Density in the Spiral Arms toward Galactic Anticenter Jia-hao Liu, Bing Liu, Rui-zhi Yang 2609.14955 Using almost 17 years of Fermi-LAT data, they isolate diffuse gamma-ray emission associated with the Local, Perseus and Outer spiral arms over 105∘<l<145∘. All three components show a clear pion bump, so the inferred emissivity is… 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Cosmic rays **Jia-hao Liu, Bing Liu, Rui-zhi Yang**\n[arXiv:2609.14955](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.14955)\n\nUsing almost **17 years of Fermi-LAT data**, they isolate diffuse gamma-ray emission associated with the Local, Perseus and Outer spiral arms over \\(105^\\circ<l<145^\\circ\\). All three components show a clear **pion bump**, so the inferred emissivity is directly tracing the underlying CR-proton population rather than relying on a purely phenomenological gamma-ray spectrum.\n\nThey obtain proton spectral indices approximately\n\\[\n\\Gamma_p\\simeq\n2.75\\quad{\\rm Local},\\qquad\n2.55\\quad{\\rm Perseus},\\qquad\n2.80\\quad{\\rm Outer},\n\\]\nwith CR energy densities above \\(10\\) GeV of order\n\\[\nu_{\\rm CR}\\sim0.2~{\\rm eV\\,cm^{-3}}\n\\]\nin all three arms. The important tension is that the outer-Galaxy CR population comes out **denser and harder than standard propagation models usually predict** as Galactocentric radius increases. Possible explanations include a larger CR halo, slower radial decline of the magnetic field, or local acceleration—especially in the Perseus arm.\n\n**Significance:** it is a clean observational handle on the radial dependence of Galactic CR transport, with obvious implications for diffuse gamma rays and Galactic-neutrino modelling.\n\n**Classification:** **genuinely useful observational CR result.**"}, {"id": "paper-2609-13363", "arxiv": "2609.13363", "title": "The Loud Tail of the Supermassive Black Hole Binary Population: Multimessenger Candidates and Prospects for SKAO", "authors": "Juhan Raidal, Ville Vaskonen, Juan Urrutia, Hardi Veermäe", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "They start from SMBH-binary population models fitted to the NANOGrav 15-year stochastic-background result and ask which already-known electromagnetic SMBH-binary candidates should populate the rare high-strain tail strongly enough to…", "section_text": "**Juhan Raidal, Ville Vaskonen, Juan Urrutia, Hardi Veermäe**\n[arXiv:2609.13363](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.13363)\n\nThey start from SMBH-binary population models fitted to the **NANOGrav 15-year stochastic-background result** and ask which already-known electromagnetic SMBH-binary candidates should populate the rare high-strain tail strongly enough to become individually resolvable PTA sources.\n\nTwo objects stand out: **3C 66B and Mrk 501**. In their population realization these systems have a good chance of rising above the unresolved stochastic background. The result is especially interesting because individual detections would discriminate between binary-evolution scenarios: strong environmental hardening changes the abundance of resolvable systems relative to a purely GW-driven inspiral population.\n\nFor the SKA era, after including confusion noise from the unresolved SMBH population, they forecast **tens of individually detectable binaries**, with 3C 66B particularly robust.\n\n**Significance:** this is genuine **multimessenger source-population physics**—connecting radio/AGN binary candidates to PTA gravitational waves rather than treating the GW background only statistically.\n\n**Classification:** **new multimessenger population forecast; conceptually strong.**", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-13363", "editionDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "The Loud Tail of the Supermassive Black Hole Binary Population: Multimessenger Candidates and Prospects for SKAO Juhan Raidal, Ville Vaskonen, Juan Urrutia, Hardi Veermäe 2609.13363 They start from SMBH-binary population models fitted to the NANOGrav 15-year stochastic-background result and ask which already-known electromagnetic SMBH-binary candidates should populate the rare high-strain tail strongly enough to… 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Multimessenger **Juhan Raidal, Ville Vaskonen, Juan Urrutia, Hardi Veermäe**\n[arXiv:2609.13363](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.13363)\n\nThey start from SMBH-binary population models fitted to the **NANOGrav 15-year stochastic-background result** and ask which already-known electromagnetic SMBH-binary candidates should populate the rare high-strain tail strongly enough to become individually resolvable PTA sources.\n\nTwo objects stand out: **3C 66B and Mrk 501**. In their population realization these systems have a good chance of rising above the unresolved stochastic background. The result is especially interesting because individual detections would discriminate between binary-evolution scenarios: strong environmental hardening changes the abundance of resolvable systems relative to a purely GW-driven inspiral population.\n\nFor the SKA era, after including confusion noise from the unresolved SMBH population, they forecast **tens of individually detectable binaries**, with 3C 66B particularly robust.\n\n**Significance:** this is genuine **multimessenger source-population physics**—connecting radio/AGN binary candidates to PTA gravitational waves rather than treating the GW background only statistically.\n\n**Classification:** **new multimessenger population forecast; conceptually strong.**"}, {"id": "paper-2609-14446", "arxiv": "2609.14446", "title": "Probe of Solar Neutrino Magnetic Moments through Spin-Flavor Precession: Resonance Structure and Antineutrino Appearance", "authors": "Pouya Bakhti, Sudip Jana, Chui-Fan Kong, Seodong Shin, Seokhoon Yun", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Oscillations"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "They perform the spin-flavor-precession problem with the full \\[ 6\\times6 \\] neutrino/antineutrino Hamiltonian and realistic GS98 and AGSS09 solar profiles.", "section_text": "**Pouya Bakhti, Sudip Jana, Chui-Fan Kong, Seodong Shin, Seokhoon Yun**\n[arXiv:2609.14446](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.14446)\n\nThey perform the spin-flavor-precession problem with the full\n\\[\n6\\times6\n\\]\nneutrino/antineutrino Hamiltonian and realistic GS98 and AGSS09 solar profiles.\n\nFor **Majorana neutrinos**, a transition magnetic moment can generate\n\\[\n\\nu_\\alpha\\rightarrow\\bar\\nu_\\beta ,\n\\]\nwhereas for Dirac neutrinos the flipped state is sterile. A useful technical result is that in normal ordering they find **no finite-density Majorana level crossing** for \\(1\\!-\\!20\\) MeV solar neutrinos. A Dirac resonance appears above about \\(12\\) MeV, but it involves only a small \\(\\nu_e\\) component, so the resonant disappearance signal is limited.\n\nThe more interesting signature is therefore **nonresonant Majorana conversion producing solar \\(\\bar\\nu_e\\)**. For a future 3-kton Jinping detector operating for \\(5\\!-\\!10\\) years they estimate sensitivity to\n\\[\nP(\\nu_e\\rightarrow\\bar\\nu_e)\n\\sim(0.85-1.3)\\times10^{-5}.\n\\]\nAssuming optimistic transverse core fields \\(B_\\perp\\sim7\\!-\\!10\\) MG, this corresponds in their \\(\\mu_{12}\\)-only example to\n\\[\n|\\mu_{12}|\\sim(2.3-4.1)\\times10^{-13}\\mu_B .\n\\]\n\n**Significance:** it gives an explicit three-flavor treatment of the distinction between **Dirac disappearance and Majorana antineutrino appearance**, which is much more informative than a simple magnetic-moment bound.\n\n**Classification:** **good neutrino-phenomenology paper; projected rather than observed result.**", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-14446", "editionDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "Probe of Solar Neutrino Magnetic Moments through Spin-Flavor Precession: Resonance Structure and Antineutrino Appearance Pouya Bakhti, Sudip Jana, Chui-Fan Kong, Seodong Shin, Seokhoon Yun 2609.14446 They perform the spin-flavor-precession problem with the full \\[ 6\\times6 \\] neutrino/antineutrino Hamiltonian and realistic GS98 and AGSS09 solar profiles. 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Neutrinos Oscillations **Pouya Bakhti, Sudip Jana, Chui-Fan Kong, Seodong Shin, Seokhoon Yun**\n[arXiv:2609.14446](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.14446)\n\nThey perform the spin-flavor-precession problem with the full\n\\[\n6\\times6\n\\]\nneutrino/antineutrino Hamiltonian and realistic GS98 and AGSS09 solar profiles.\n\nFor **Majorana neutrinos**, a transition magnetic moment can generate\n\\[\n\\nu_\\alpha\\rightarrow\\bar\\nu_\\beta ,\n\\]\nwhereas for Dirac neutrinos the flipped state is sterile. A useful technical result is that in normal ordering they find **no finite-density Majorana level crossing** for \\(1\\!-\\!20\\) MeV solar neutrinos. A Dirac resonance appears above about \\(12\\) MeV, but it involves only a small \\(\\nu_e\\) component, so the resonant disappearance signal is limited.\n\nThe more interesting signature is therefore **nonresonant Majorana conversion producing solar \\(\\bar\\nu_e\\)**. For a future 3-kton Jinping detector operating for \\(5\\!-\\!10\\) years they estimate sensitivity to\n\\[\nP(\\nu_e\\rightarrow\\bar\\nu_e)\n\\sim(0.85-1.3)\\times10^{-5}.\n\\]\nAssuming optimistic transverse core fields \\(B_\\perp\\sim7\\!-\\!10\\) MG, this corresponds in their \\(\\mu_{12}\\)-only example to\n\\[\n|\\mu_{12}|\\sim(2.3-4.1)\\times10^{-13}\\mu_B .\n\\]\n\n**Significance:** it gives an explicit three-flavor treatment of the distinction between **Dirac disappearance and Majorana antineutrino appearance**, which is much more informative than a simple magnetic-moment bound.\n\n**Classification:** **good neutrino-phenomenology paper; projected rather than observed result.**"}, {"id": "paper-2609-13821", "arxiv": "2609.13821", "title": "Consistent treatment of flavour-specific neutrino self-interactions in cosmology", "authors": "Gabriela Barenboim, Marco Drewes, Isabel M. Oldengott", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Oscillations"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This paper addresses a subtlety that is often glossed over in cosmological constraints on secret neutrino interactions: if the new interaction acts only on a particular flavor, neutrino oscillations/mixing mean that one cannot simply…", "section_text": "**Gabriela Barenboim, Marco Drewes, Isabel M. Oldengott**\n[arXiv:2609.13821](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.13821)\n\nThis paper addresses a subtlety that is often glossed over in cosmological constraints on secret neutrino interactions: if the new interaction acts only on a particular **flavor**, neutrino oscillations/mixing mean that one cannot simply evolve three independent flavor fluids.\n\nStarting from the neutrino **quantum kinetic equations**, they derive the Boltzmann hierarchy in the neutrino-mass basis and determine when a simpler combined-fluid approximation is valid.\n\nTheir practical result is neat. Existing CMB limits on an effective neutrino self-coupling should be weakened by approximately\n\\[\n\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\nwhen two flavors interact and by approximately\n\\[\n\\sqrt{6}\n\\]\nwhen only one flavor interacts. The \\(\\nu_e\\)-only case is the one where the fluid approximation becomes somewhat less reliable.\n\n**Significance:** this is one of those papers that can quietly change how constraints are recast across a large class of neutrino-BSM models; the correction is not orders of magnitude, but it is conceptually important.\n\n**Classification:** **methodological improvement to cosmological neutrino constraints.**", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-13821", "editionDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "Consistent treatment of flavour-specific neutrino self-interactions in cosmology Gabriela Barenboim, Marco Drewes, Isabel M. Oldengott 2609.13821 This paper addresses a subtlety that is often glossed over in cosmological constraints on secret neutrino interactions: if the new interaction acts only on a particular flavor, neutrino oscillations/mixing mean that one cannot simply… 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Neutrinos Oscillations **Gabriela Barenboim, Marco Drewes, Isabel M. Oldengott**\n[arXiv:2609.13821](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.13821)\n\nThis paper addresses a subtlety that is often glossed over in cosmological constraints on secret neutrino interactions: if the new interaction acts only on a particular **flavor**, neutrino oscillations/mixing mean that one cannot simply evolve three independent flavor fluids.\n\nStarting from the neutrino **quantum kinetic equations**, they derive the Boltzmann hierarchy in the neutrino-mass basis and determine when a simpler combined-fluid approximation is valid.\n\nTheir practical result is neat. Existing CMB limits on an effective neutrino self-coupling should be weakened by approximately\n\\[\n\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\nwhen two flavors interact and by approximately\n\\[\n\\sqrt{6}\n\\]\nwhen only one flavor interacts. The \\(\\nu_e\\)-only case is the one where the fluid approximation becomes somewhat less reliable.\n\n**Significance:** this is one of those papers that can quietly change how constraints are recast across a large class of neutrino-BSM models; the correction is not orders of magnitude, but it is conceptually important.\n\n**Classification:** **methodological improvement to cosmological neutrino constraints.**"}, {"id": "paper-2609-15970", "arxiv": "2609.15970", "title": "Less isn't more: Cosmological bounds on the neutrino masses are robust to changes in the neutrino abundance", "authors": "Sebastian J. Figueroa, Alexander C. Sobotka, Adrienne L. Erickcek", "tags": ["Neutrinos"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This asks a timely question: can the increasingly aggressive cosmological limit on \\[ \\sum m_\\nu \\] be evaded by changing the relic neutrino abundance through a nonstandard post-BBN thermal history?", "section_text": "**Sebastian J. Figueroa, Alexander C. Sobotka, Adrienne L. Erickcek**\n[arXiv:2609.15970](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.15970)\n\nThis asks a timely question: can the increasingly aggressive cosmological limit on\n\\[\n\\sum m_\\nu\n\\]\nbe evaded by changing the relic neutrino abundance through a nonstandard post-BBN thermal history?\n\nThey explicitly model late particle decays injecting photons and/or dark radiation, and then perform MCMC analyses using **Planck primary CMB, Planck+ACT lensing and DESI BAO**. Surprisingly, reducing the neutrino abundance does **not** substantially weaken the mass bound.\n\nFor degenerate masses they quote\n\\[\n\\sum m_\\nu <0.0691~{\\rm eV}\n\\]\nfor the standard thermal history,\n\\[\n<0.0652~{\\rm eV}\n\\]\nfor photon-only injection, and\n\\[\n<0.0710~{\\rm eV}\n\\]\nwhen dark radiation is also produced. Even allowing the neutrino/photon temperature ratio to vary phenomenologically gives only\n\\[\n\\sum m_\\nu<0.0724~{\\rm eV}\n\\]\nat 95% credibility.\n\nThe underlying reason is interesting: \\(T_\\nu\\) and \\(\\sum m_\\nu\\) come out **positively correlated** once the full cosmological dataset is used.\n\n**Significance:** the emerging tension between cosmological neutrino-mass bounds and laboratory oscillation mass splittings cannot apparently be dismissed simply by saying “perhaps the relic neutrino density is smaller.”\n\n**Classification:** **important robustness check.**", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-15970", "editionDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "Less isn't more: Cosmological bounds on the neutrino masses are robust to changes in the neutrino abundance Sebastian J. Figueroa, Alexander C. Sobotka, Adrienne L. Erickcek 2609.15970 This asks a timely question: can the increasingly aggressive cosmological limit on \\[ \\sum m_\\nu \\] be evaded by changing the relic neutrino abundance through a nonstandard post-BBN thermal history? 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Neutrinos **Sebastian J. Figueroa, Alexander C. Sobotka, Adrienne L. Erickcek**\n[arXiv:2609.15970](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.15970)\n\nThis asks a timely question: can the increasingly aggressive cosmological limit on\n\\[\n\\sum m_\\nu\n\\]\nbe evaded by changing the relic neutrino abundance through a nonstandard post-BBN thermal history?\n\nThey explicitly model late particle decays injecting photons and/or dark radiation, and then perform MCMC analyses using **Planck primary CMB, Planck+ACT lensing and DESI BAO**. Surprisingly, reducing the neutrino abundance does **not** substantially weaken the mass bound.\n\nFor degenerate masses they quote\n\\[\n\\sum m_\\nu <0.0691~{\\rm eV}\n\\]\nfor the standard thermal history,\n\\[\n<0.0652~{\\rm eV}\n\\]\nfor photon-only injection, and\n\\[\n<0.0710~{\\rm eV}\n\\]\nwhen dark radiation is also produced. Even allowing the neutrino/photon temperature ratio to vary phenomenologically gives only\n\\[\n\\sum m_\\nu<0.0724~{\\rm eV}\n\\]\nat 95% credibility.\n\nThe underlying reason is interesting: \\(T_\\nu\\) and \\(\\sum m_\\nu\\) come out **positively correlated** once the full cosmological dataset is used.\n\n**Significance:** the emerging tension between cosmological neutrino-mass bounds and laboratory oscillation mass splittings cannot apparently be dismissed simply by saying “perhaps the relic neutrino density is smaller.”\n\n**Classification:** **important robustness check.**"}, {"id": "paper-2609-14508", "arxiv": "2609.14508", "title": "First Light of Neutron Star Mergers: Off-axis Cocoon Cooling X-ray Emission from Short Gamma-Ray Burst Jets", "authors": "Jian-He Zheng, Wenbin Lu", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The problem is straightforward: short-GRB jets are strongly beamed, so most neutron-star mergers will not show prompt gamma rays to an off-axis observer. The authors investigate whether the jet cocoon provides a less-beamed early…", "section_text": "**Jian-He Zheng, Wenbin Lu**\n[arXiv:2609.14508](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.14508)\n\nThe problem is straightforward: short-GRB jets are strongly beamed, so most neutron-star mergers will not show prompt gamma rays to an off-axis observer. The authors investigate whether the **jet cocoon** provides a less-beamed early counterpart.\n\nHydrodynamic simulations plus radiative post-processing give a mildly relativistic cocoon producing soft quasi-thermal X-rays with\n\\[\nL_X\\sim10^{46}-10^{48}\\ {\\rm erg\\,s^{-1}},\n\\qquad\nkT\\sim0.1-1~{\\rm keV},\n\\]\nlasting a few to roughly ten seconds.\n\nFor viewing angles around \\(10^\\circ\\), Einstein Probe could see the signal to \\(z\\sim0.3\\). For a GW170817-like nearby event, detectability may extend to roughly \\(45^\\circ\\). Their fiducial Einstein Probe rate is about\n\\[\n0.5~{\\rm yr}^{-1}.\n\\]\n\n**Significance:** a genuinely useful possible **early X-ray trigger for off-axis GW mergers**, preceding the kilonova and conventional afterglow.\n\n**Classification:** **multimessenger prediction, not yet observational confirmation.**", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-14508", "editionDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "First Light of Neutron Star Mergers: Off-axis Cocoon Cooling X-ray Emission from Short Gamma-Ray Burst Jets Jian-He Zheng, Wenbin Lu 2609.14508 The problem is straightforward: short-GRB jets are strongly beamed, so most neutron-star mergers will not show prompt gamma rays to an off-axis observer. The authors investigate whether the jet cocoon provides a less-beamed early… 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Gamma rays Multimessenger **Jian-He Zheng, Wenbin Lu**\n[arXiv:2609.14508](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.14508)\n\nThe problem is straightforward: short-GRB jets are strongly beamed, so most neutron-star mergers will not show prompt gamma rays to an off-axis observer. The authors investigate whether the **jet cocoon** provides a less-beamed early counterpart.\n\nHydrodynamic simulations plus radiative post-processing give a mildly relativistic cocoon producing soft quasi-thermal X-rays with\n\\[\nL_X\\sim10^{46}-10^{48}\\ {\\rm erg\\,s^{-1}},\n\\qquad\nkT\\sim0.1-1~{\\rm keV},\n\\]\nlasting a few to roughly ten seconds.\n\nFor viewing angles around \\(10^\\circ\\), Einstein Probe could see the signal to \\(z\\sim0.3\\). For a GW170817-like nearby event, detectability may extend to roughly \\(45^\\circ\\). Their fiducial Einstein Probe rate is about\n\\[\n0.5~{\\rm yr}^{-1}.\n\\]\n\n**Significance:** a genuinely useful possible **early X-ray trigger for off-axis GW mergers**, preceding the kilonova and conventional afterglow.\n\n**Classification:** **multimessenger prediction, not yet observational confirmation.**"}, {"id": "paper-2609-14551", "arxiv": "2609.14551", "title": "First results on the search for the Galactic Center Excess in the sub-GeV band with the emulsion telescope in GRAINE 2023", "authors": "Yuya Nakamura et al.", "tags": ["Gamma rays"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "GRAINE is interesting because the balloon-borne emulsion gamma-ray telescope reaches angular resolutions of about \\[ 1^\\circ\\ {\\rm at}\\ 100~{\\rm MeV}, \\qquad 0.1^\\circ\\ {\\rm at}\\ 1~{\\rm GeV}, \\] making the difficult sub-GeV…", "section_text": "**Yuya Nakamura et al.**\n[arXiv:2609.14551](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.14551)\n\nGRAINE is interesting because the balloon-borne emulsion gamma-ray telescope reaches angular resolutions of about\n\\[\n1^\\circ\\ {\\rm at}\\ 100~{\\rm MeV},\n\\qquad\n0.1^\\circ\\ {\\rm at}\\ 1~{\\rm GeV},\n\\]\nmaking the difficult sub-GeV Galactic-center region less confusion-limited than Fermi in some respects.\n\nRather than looking at the familiar \\(\\sim2\\) GeV GCE peak, they deliberately examine\n\\[\n75-300~{\\rm MeV},\n\\]\nwhere predicted spectra from millisecond-pulsar and DM-annihilation interpretations diverge more strongly.\n\nThey see **no significant excess** and obtain a \\(2\\sigma\\) upper limit\n\\[\nE^2\\Phi_{\\gamma}<1.70\\times10^{-7}\n\\ {\\rm GeV\\,cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}\n\\]\nfor a \\(1^\\circ\\)-radius Galactic-center ROI. Current statistics still allow both the DM and MSP interpretations.\n\n**Significance:** the important part is not today's limit itself, which is still weak, but the ability to obtain **high-angular-resolution sub-GeV imaging** precisely where the GCE models become spectrally distinguishable.\n\n**Classification:** **first instrumental result; interesting but not yet constraining enough to settle the GCE.**", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-14551", "editionDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "First results on the search for the Galactic Center Excess in the sub-GeV band with the emulsion telescope in GRAINE 2023 Yuya Nakamura et al. 2609.14551 GRAINE is interesting because the balloon-borne emulsion gamma-ray telescope reaches angular resolutions of about \\[ 1^\\circ\\ {\\rm at}\\ 100~{\\rm MeV}, \\qquad 0.1^\\circ\\ {\\rm at}\\ 1~{\\rm GeV}, \\] making the difficult sub-GeV… 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Gamma rays **Yuya Nakamura et al.**\n[arXiv:2609.14551](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.14551)\n\nGRAINE is interesting because the balloon-borne emulsion gamma-ray telescope reaches angular resolutions of about\n\\[\n1^\\circ\\ {\\rm at}\\ 100~{\\rm MeV},\n\\qquad\n0.1^\\circ\\ {\\rm at}\\ 1~{\\rm GeV},\n\\]\nmaking the difficult sub-GeV Galactic-center region less confusion-limited than Fermi in some respects.\n\nRather than looking at the familiar \\(\\sim2\\) GeV GCE peak, they deliberately examine\n\\[\n75-300~{\\rm MeV},\n\\]\nwhere predicted spectra from millisecond-pulsar and DM-annihilation interpretations diverge more strongly.\n\nThey see **no significant excess** and obtain a \\(2\\sigma\\) upper limit\n\\[\nE^2\\Phi_{\\gamma}<1.70\\times10^{-7}\n\\ {\\rm GeV\\,cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}\n\\]\nfor a \\(1^\\circ\\)-radius Galactic-center ROI. Current statistics still allow both the DM and MSP interpretations.\n\n**Significance:** the important part is not today's limit itself, which is still weak, but the ability to obtain **high-angular-resolution sub-GeV imaging** precisely where the GCE models become spectrally distinguishable.\n\n**Classification:** **first instrumental result; interesting but not yet constraining enough to settle the GCE.**"}, {"id": "paper-2609-15985", "arxiv": "2609.15985", "title": "Inferring dark matter masses and interactions from high recoil energy events in LUX-ZEPLIN", "authors": "Stefano Palmisano, Michele Tammaro, Andrea Tesi", "tags": ["Dark matter"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "There is now a flood of papers trying to model-build around the LZ 248 keV recoil candidate. This one is more useful than most because it is relatively model-independent.", "section_text": "**Stefano Palmisano, Michele Tammaro, Andrea Tesi**\n[arXiv:2609.15985](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.15985)\n\nThere is now a flood of papers trying to model-build around the LZ \\(248\\) keV recoil candidate. This one is more useful than most because it is relatively **model-independent**.\n\nThey infer the posterior directly in the space of DM masses, mass splittings and effective nuclear interactions, retaining exact two-body kinematics. The single high-recoil event plus the lack of lower-energy events drives the solution into disconnected kinematic regimes.\n\nGeneric interactions favor a finely tuned **endothermic heavy-WIMP region**, while exothermic scattering permits masses ranging from sub-GeV to heavy DM with large splittings. Purely elastic interpretations survive only with momentum suppression and/or spin-dependent operators.\n\n**Significance:** the inference characterizes what the LZ candidate implies without committing to a specific \\(U(1)'\\), SUSY or freeze-in construction.\n\n**Classification:** **useful inference paper, but the underlying LZ anomaly is still weak; not evidence for DM.**\n\nAn additional methodological paper is **“A Unified Numerical Study of Axion Stars: From the Nonrelativistic Regime to General Relativity”** by Arabameri, Arias, Colipí-Marchant and Schiappacasse. They benchmark Schrödinger–Poisson, relativistic EFT and full Einstein–Klein–Gordon calculations against one another and show quantitatively where the usual nonrelativistic axion-star description breaks down. This is a reference and methodological study.", "path": "2026/09/15/index.html#paper-2609-15985", "editionDate": "15 September 2026", "searchText": "Inferring dark matter masses and interactions from high recoil energy events in LUX-ZEPLIN Stefano Palmisano, Michele Tammaro, Andrea Tesi 2609.15985 There is now a flood of papers trying to model-build around the LZ 248 keV recoil candidate. This one is more useful than most because it is relatively model-independent. 2026-09-15 15 September 2026 Dark matter **Stefano Palmisano, Michele Tammaro, Andrea Tesi**\n[arXiv:2609.15985](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.15985)\n\nThere is now a flood of papers trying to model-build around the LZ \\(248\\) keV recoil candidate. This one is more useful than most because it is relatively **model-independent**.\n\nThey infer the posterior directly in the space of DM masses, mass splittings and effective nuclear interactions, retaining exact two-body kinematics. The single high-recoil event plus the lack of lower-energy events drives the solution into disconnected kinematic regimes.\n\nGeneric interactions favor a finely tuned **endothermic heavy-WIMP region**, while exothermic scattering permits masses ranging from sub-GeV to heavy DM with large splittings. Purely elastic interpretations survive only with momentum suppression and/or spin-dependent operators.\n\n**Significance:** the inference characterizes what the LZ candidate implies without committing to a specific \\(U(1)'\\), SUSY or freeze-in construction.\n\n**Classification:** **useful inference paper, but the underlying LZ anomaly is still weak; not evidence for DM.**\n\nAn additional methodological paper is **“A Unified Numerical Study of Axion Stars: From the Nonrelativistic Regime to General Relativity”** by Arabameri, Arias, Colipí-Marchant and Schiappacasse. They benchmark Schrödinger–Poisson, relativistic EFT and full Einstein–Klein–Gordon calculations against one another and show quantitatively where the usual nonrelativistic axion-star description breaks down. This is a reference and methodological study."}, {"id": "paper-2609-12055", "arxiv": "2609.12055", "title": "Time-Integrated Searches for Sub-TeV Neutrino Sources with IceCube-DeepCore", "authors": "IceCube Collaboration — R. Abbasi et al.", "tags": ["Neutrinos"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "IceCube has developed a dedicated sub-TeV point-source analysis using 11.1 years of DeepCore data, extending source searches down into an energy range that ordinary IceCube point-source analyses do not exploit efficiently. The…", "section_text": "**IceCube Collaboration — R. Abbasi et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.12055](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12055)\n\nIceCube has developed a dedicated **sub-TeV point-source analysis using 11.1 years of DeepCore data**, extending source searches down into an energy range that ordinary IceCube point-source analyses do not exploit efficiently. The motivation is especially strong for sources with soft spectra.\n\nThey perform three searches: X-ray-bright AGN selected with Swift/BAT, including **NGC 1068**; Galactic Fermi-LAT sources whose spectra look compatible with \\(\\pi^0\\) decay; and an all-sky point-source search. They also combine individual source \\(p\\)-values with a binomial test to look for weak population-level emission. No statistically significant sub-TeV signal appears in any of them.\n\nFor NGC 1068, assuming\n\n\\[\n\\frac{d\\Phi_\\nu}{dE}\\propto E^{-3.4},\n\\]\n\nthey obtain\n\n\\[\n\\left.\\Phi_{\\nu+\\bar\\nu}\\right|_{1\\,{\\rm TeV}}\n<\n9.5\\times10^{-11}\n\\ {\\rm TeV^{-1}\\,cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}\n\\]\n\nper flavor at 90% C.L., probing approximately **30–400 GeV**. Importantly, that upper limit is still about a factor of two *above* the extrapolation of IceCube's higher-energy NGC 1068 measurement, so it does **not yet rule out** a simple continuation of the measured soft component.\n\n**Significance:** this provides an observational input for multimessenger modelling of NGC 1068. It begins constraining what the neutrino spectrum can do below the existing IceCube measurement and therefore interfaces naturally with MeV–GeV electromagnetic constraints and source-opacity/cascade models.\n\n**Assessment:** **genuinely new experimental result.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12055", "editionDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Time-Integrated Searches for Sub-TeV Neutrino Sources with IceCube-DeepCore IceCube Collaboration — R. Abbasi et al. 2609.12055 IceCube has developed a dedicated sub-TeV point-source analysis using 11.1 years of DeepCore data, extending source searches down into an energy range that ordinary IceCube point-source analyses do not exploit efficiently. The… 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Neutrinos **IceCube Collaboration — R. Abbasi et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.12055](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12055)\n\nIceCube has developed a dedicated **sub-TeV point-source analysis using 11.1 years of DeepCore data**, extending source searches down into an energy range that ordinary IceCube point-source analyses do not exploit efficiently. The motivation is especially strong for sources with soft spectra.\n\nThey perform three searches: X-ray-bright AGN selected with Swift/BAT, including **NGC 1068**; Galactic Fermi-LAT sources whose spectra look compatible with \\(\\pi^0\\) decay; and an all-sky point-source search. They also combine individual source \\(p\\)-values with a binomial test to look for weak population-level emission. No statistically significant sub-TeV signal appears in any of them.\n\nFor NGC 1068, assuming\n\n\\[\n\\frac{d\\Phi_\\nu}{dE}\\propto E^{-3.4},\n\\]\n\nthey obtain\n\n\\[\n\\left.\\Phi_{\\nu+\\bar\\nu}\\right|_{1\\,{\\rm TeV}}\n<\n9.5\\times10^{-11}\n\\ {\\rm TeV^{-1}\\,cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}\n\\]\n\nper flavor at 90% C.L., probing approximately **30–400 GeV**. Importantly, that upper limit is still about a factor of two *above* the extrapolation of IceCube's higher-energy NGC 1068 measurement, so it does **not yet rule out** a simple continuation of the measured soft component.\n\n**Significance:** this provides an observational input for multimessenger modelling of NGC 1068. It begins constraining what the neutrino spectrum can do below the existing IceCube measurement and therefore interfaces naturally with MeV–GeV electromagnetic constraints and source-opacity/cascade models.\n\n**Assessment:** **genuinely new experimental result.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-12174", "arxiv": "2609.12174", "title": "Hidden structures in the UHE sky: unveiling potential sources via spectral signatures", "authors": "Mateus Zeferino Rennó, Cainã de Oliveira", "tags": ["Cosmic rays"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This is conceptually simple but potentially quite useful.", "section_text": "**Mateus Zeferino Rennó, Cainã de Oliveira**  \n[arXiv:2609.12174](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12174)\n\nThis is conceptually simple but potentially quite useful.\n\nMost UHECR source searches ask whether some angular region contains **more particles than expected**,\n\n\\[\nN(\\hat n)>N_{\\rm iso}(\\hat n).\n\\]\n\nBut a real source contribution could alter the *local energy distribution* without producing an obvious flux excess, particularly after magnetic smearing.\n\nThe authors construct a directional functional \\(\\mu_E\\) of the energy spectrum specifically designed to separate\n\n\\[\n\\text{flux normalization}\n\\qquad\\text{from}\\qquad\n\\text{spectral shape}.\n\\]\n\nThey then generalize the **Li–Ma significance** formalism to such energy-weighted observables and test the statistic in Monte Carlo. Hard-spectrum patches that are essentially invisible in conventional event-count maps become detectable with \\(\\mu_E\\).\n\nThat idea is interesting because magnetic deflections can smear angular overdensities while still allowing a direction-dependent spectrum to survive.\n\n**Significance:** it is complementary to conventional UHECR anisotropy statistics and particularly relevant to the general question of how much source information survives GMF/EGMF smearing. Comparison with composition-sensitive and entropy/information-based approaches could help establish the statistic's complementary value.\n\n**Assessment:** **new methodology; potentially useful rather than yet experimentally decisive.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12174", "editionDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Hidden structures in the UHE sky: unveiling potential sources via spectral signatures Mateus Zeferino Rennó, Cainã de Oliveira 2609.12174 This is conceptually simple but potentially quite useful. 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Cosmic rays **Mateus Zeferino Rennó, Cainã de Oliveira**  \n[arXiv:2609.12174](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12174)\n\nThis is conceptually simple but potentially quite useful.\n\nMost UHECR source searches ask whether some angular region contains **more particles than expected**,\n\n\\[\nN(\\hat n)>N_{\\rm iso}(\\hat n).\n\\]\n\nBut a real source contribution could alter the *local energy distribution* without producing an obvious flux excess, particularly after magnetic smearing.\n\nThe authors construct a directional functional \\(\\mu_E\\) of the energy spectrum specifically designed to separate\n\n\\[\n\\text{flux normalization}\n\\qquad\\text{from}\\qquad\n\\text{spectral shape}.\n\\]\n\nThey then generalize the **Li–Ma significance** formalism to such energy-weighted observables and test the statistic in Monte Carlo. Hard-spectrum patches that are essentially invisible in conventional event-count maps become detectable with \\(\\mu_E\\).\n\nThat idea is interesting because magnetic deflections can smear angular overdensities while still allowing a direction-dependent spectrum to survive.\n\n**Significance:** it is complementary to conventional UHECR anisotropy statistics and particularly relevant to the general question of how much source information survives GMF/EGMF smearing. Comparison with composition-sensitive and entropy/information-based approaches could help establish the statistic's complementary value.\n\n**Assessment:** **new methodology; potentially useful rather than yet experimentally decisive.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-12044", "arxiv": "2609.12044", "title": "Constraining dark matter using 20-year INTEGRAL/IBIS observations I: Primordial black holes", "authors": "Jordan Koechler, Pedro De la Torre Luque", "tags": ["Dark matter", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This is a nice astroparticle-analysis paper.", "section_text": "**Jordan Koechler, Pedro De la Torre Luque**  \n[arXiv:2609.12044](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12044)\n\nThis is a nice astroparticle-analysis paper.\n\nLow-mass PBHs emit \\(e^\\pm\\) through Hawking evaporation. Those electrons and positrons subsequently inverse-Compton scatter the Galactic radiation field,\n\n\\[\ne^\\pm+\\gamma_{\\rm ISRF}\\rightarrow e^\\pm+\\gamma ,\n\\]\n\ncreating diffuse hard X-rays.\n\nThe authors use **20 years of INTEGRAL/IBIS observations** and, importantly, do not rely only on the energy spectrum. They perform a combined **spectral + morphological analysis**, modelling the conventional Galactic hard-X-ray sky with cosmic-ray-electron IC emission plus unresolved accreting white dwarfs and adding the PBH-induced IC morphology.\n\nThey obtain competitive constraints on the PBH DM fraction for monochromatic, extended and rotating-PBH mass functions. Their dominant theoretical uncertainty is not Hawking physics itself but the Galactic transport of the relatively low-energy \\(e^\\pm\\) responsible for the IC emission.\n\nThat is an important methodological point:\n\n\\[\n\\text{particle-physics injection}\n\\longrightarrow e^\\pm\\ {\\rm propagation}\n\\longrightarrow {\\rm IC}\n\\longrightarrow {\\rm diffuse\\ morphology}.\n\\]\n\n**Significance:** besides the PBH result itself, the spectral+morphological separation of signal from Galactic diffuse emission is highly relevant to any MeV/hard-X-ray multimessenger analysis.\n\n**Assessment:** **genuinely new search strategy and observational constraint.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12044", "editionDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Constraining dark matter using 20-year INTEGRAL/IBIS observations I: Primordial black holes Jordan Koechler, Pedro De la Torre Luque 2609.12044 This is a nice astroparticle-analysis paper. 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Dark matter Multimessenger **Jordan Koechler, Pedro De la Torre Luque**  \n[arXiv:2609.12044](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12044)\n\nThis is a nice astroparticle-analysis paper.\n\nLow-mass PBHs emit \\(e^\\pm\\) through Hawking evaporation. Those electrons and positrons subsequently inverse-Compton scatter the Galactic radiation field,\n\n\\[\ne^\\pm+\\gamma_{\\rm ISRF}\\rightarrow e^\\pm+\\gamma ,\n\\]\n\ncreating diffuse hard X-rays.\n\nThe authors use **20 years of INTEGRAL/IBIS observations** and, importantly, do not rely only on the energy spectrum. They perform a combined **spectral + morphological analysis**, modelling the conventional Galactic hard-X-ray sky with cosmic-ray-electron IC emission plus unresolved accreting white dwarfs and adding the PBH-induced IC morphology.\n\nThey obtain competitive constraints on the PBH DM fraction for monochromatic, extended and rotating-PBH mass functions. Their dominant theoretical uncertainty is not Hawking physics itself but the Galactic transport of the relatively low-energy \\(e^\\pm\\) responsible for the IC emission.\n\nThat is an important methodological point:\n\n\\[\n\\text{particle-physics injection}\n\\longrightarrow e^\\pm\\ {\\rm propagation}\n\\longrightarrow {\\rm IC}\n\\longrightarrow {\\rm diffuse\\ morphology}.\n\\]\n\n**Significance:** besides the PBH result itself, the spectral+morphological separation of signal from Galactic diffuse emission is highly relevant to any MeV/hard-X-ray multimessenger analysis.\n\n**Assessment:** **genuinely new search strategy and observational constraint.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-12821", "arxiv": "2609.12821", "title": "Supernova cooling from neutrinophilic dark matter", "authors": "Yugen Lin", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This paper considers light DM whose dominant coupling is to neutrinos and asks whether it can be produced inside a core-collapse SN through neutrino–neutrino reactions.", "section_text": "**Yugen Lin**  \n[arXiv:2609.12821](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12821)\n\nThis paper considers light DM whose dominant coupling is to neutrinos and asks whether it can be produced inside a core-collapse SN through neutrino–neutrino reactions.\n\nBecause the proto-neutron-star core contains an enormous neutrino density, even extremely weak interactions can efficiently populate a new light sector. If the produced DM escapes, it adds a new cooling channel.\n\nUsing modern supernova simulation profiles, the paper derives bounds on effective \\(\\nu\\)-DM interactions for sub-GeV DM. The striking numerical claim is that for\n\n\\[\nm_\\chi\\lesssim {\\cal O}(100)\\ {\\rm MeV},\n\\]\n\nthe SN cooling limits on the reference DM–neutrino cross section can improve existing indirect-detection limits by **more than ten orders of magnitude**.\n\nThe physics is essentially the usual SN energy-loss argument, but with the very large thermal neutrino bath acting as the production environment rather than nucleons/electrons.\n\n**Significance:** it connects supernova neutrino transport directly to light-dark-sector phenomenology and illustrates how dramatically SN1987A-like arguments can outperform terrestrial/indirect searches for genuinely neutrinophilic states.\n\n**Assessment:** **new BSM constraint; quite interesting.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12821", "editionDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Supernova cooling from neutrinophilic dark matter Yugen Lin 2609.12821 This paper considers light DM whose dominant coupling is to neutrinos and asks whether it can be produced inside a core-collapse SN through neutrino–neutrino reactions. 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter BSM **Yugen Lin**  \n[arXiv:2609.12821](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12821)\n\nThis paper considers light DM whose dominant coupling is to neutrinos and asks whether it can be produced inside a core-collapse SN through neutrino–neutrino reactions.\n\nBecause the proto-neutron-star core contains an enormous neutrino density, even extremely weak interactions can efficiently populate a new light sector. If the produced DM escapes, it adds a new cooling channel.\n\nUsing modern supernova simulation profiles, the paper derives bounds on effective \\(\\nu\\)-DM interactions for sub-GeV DM. The striking numerical claim is that for\n\n\\[\nm_\\chi\\lesssim {\\cal O}(100)\\ {\\rm MeV},\n\\]\n\nthe SN cooling limits on the reference DM–neutrino cross section can improve existing indirect-detection limits by **more than ten orders of magnitude**.\n\nThe physics is essentially the usual SN energy-loss argument, but with the very large thermal neutrino bath acting as the production environment rather than nucleons/electrons.\n\n**Significance:** it connects supernova neutrino transport directly to light-dark-sector phenomenology and illustrates how dramatically SN1987A-like arguments can outperform terrestrial/indirect searches for genuinely neutrinophilic states.\n\n**Assessment:** **new BSM constraint; quite interesting.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-12942", "arxiv": "2609.12942", "title": "Probing Neutrinophilic Axion-Like Particles in Tritium Beta Decay", "authors": "Yi Chung, Florian Goertz, Maya Hager, Joscha Lauer", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Here the new particle is a keV-scale ALP coupling preferentially to neutrinos. Such a state modifies the ordinary tritium spectrum measured by KATRIN and potentially by TRISTAN.", "section_text": "**Yi Chung, Florian Goertz, Maya Hager, Joscha Lauer**  \n[arXiv:2609.12942](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12942)\n\nHere the new particle is a keV-scale ALP coupling preferentially to neutrinos. Such a state modifies the ordinary tritium spectrum measured by KATRIN and potentially by **TRISTAN**.\n\nThe authors calculate the spectral distortions for both lepton-number-conserving and lepton-number-violating interactions and derive updated laboratory constraints. Ordinarily cosmology wins over beta-decay experiments, but they emphasize an important assumption behind that comparison: the particle and its interactions must exist in essentially the same form in the early Universe.\n\nThey therefore construct an explicit pseudo-Nambu–Goldstone realization with a nontrivial symmetry-breaking thermal history in which the usual cosmological constraints can be evaded. In that case,\n\n\\[\n{\\rm KATRIN/TRISTAN}\n\\]\n\ncan become the leading probe.\n\n**Significance:** good example of the often-overlooked distinction between a powerful cosmological constraint and a genuinely model-independent laboratory constraint.\n\n**Assessment:** **interesting neutrino/ALP phenomenology.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12942", "editionDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Probing Neutrinophilic Axion-Like Particles in Tritium Beta Decay Yi Chung, Florian Goertz, Maya Hager, Joscha Lauer 2609.12942 Here the new particle is a keV-scale ALP coupling preferentially to neutrinos. Such a state modifies the ordinary tritium spectrum measured by KATRIN and potentially by TRISTAN. 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter BSM **Yi Chung, Florian Goertz, Maya Hager, Joscha Lauer**  \n[arXiv:2609.12942](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12942)\n\nHere the new particle is a keV-scale ALP coupling preferentially to neutrinos. Such a state modifies the ordinary tritium spectrum measured by KATRIN and potentially by **TRISTAN**.\n\nThe authors calculate the spectral distortions for both lepton-number-conserving and lepton-number-violating interactions and derive updated laboratory constraints. Ordinarily cosmology wins over beta-decay experiments, but they emphasize an important assumption behind that comparison: the particle and its interactions must exist in essentially the same form in the early Universe.\n\nThey therefore construct an explicit pseudo-Nambu–Goldstone realization with a nontrivial symmetry-breaking thermal history in which the usual cosmological constraints can be evaded. In that case,\n\n\\[\n{\\rm KATRIN/TRISTAN}\n\\]\n\ncan become the leading probe.\n\n**Significance:** good example of the often-overlooked distinction between a powerful cosmological constraint and a genuinely model-independent laboratory constraint.\n\n**Assessment:** **interesting neutrino/ALP phenomenology.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-12542", "arxiv": "2609.12542", "title": "Leptogenesis Determined By Low Energy Parameters", "authors": "Xiao-Gang He, Zhong-Lv Huang, Raymond R. Volkas, Yu-Qi Xiao", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This attacks the usual problem with type-I seesaw leptogenesis: the heavy-neutrino sector normally contains enough freedom that low-energy neutrino observables provide little information about baryogenesis.", "section_text": "**Xiao-Gang He, Zhong-Lv Huang, Raymond R. Volkas, Yu-Qi Xiao**  \n[arXiv:2609.12542](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12542)\n\nThis attacks the usual problem with type-I seesaw leptogenesis: the heavy-neutrino sector normally contains enough freedom that low-energy neutrino observables provide little information about baryogenesis.\n\nThey instead impose three predictive relations,\n\n\\[\nM_D=M_u,\\qquad M_D=M_d,\\qquad \\text{or}\\qquad M_D=M_\\ell,\n\\]\n\nso the seesaw equation allows the heavy Majorana matrix to be reconstructed from low-energy parameters.\n\nThey evolve the flavored asymmetries using **density-matrix Boltzmann equations**. Viable thermal leptogenesis occurs for normal ordering with nonzero Majorana phases in the down-quark-like and charged-lepton-like constructions. Successful points favor a close heavy-neutrino pair,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|M_i-M_j|}{M_i}<10^{-3},\n\\]\n\nalthough the authors stress that it is not the conventional resonant-leptogenesis limit.\n\nThe interesting experimental payoff is \\(0\\nu\\beta\\beta\\): some successful leptogenesis regions predict\n\n\\[\nm_{\\beta\\beta}\n\\]\n\nwithin reach of future **sub-10-meV** experiments such as LEGEND-1000, nEXO and CUPID-1T.\n\n**Significance:** this is exactly the kind of seesaw construction where cosmological baryogenesis and measurable low-energy neutrino observables cease to be almost completely disconnected.\n\n**Assessment:** **good neutrino-pheno paper; model-dependent but predictive.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12542", "editionDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Leptogenesis Determined By Low Energy Parameters Xiao-Gang He, Zhong-Lv Huang, Raymond R. Volkas, Yu-Qi Xiao 2609.12542 This attacks the usual problem with type-I seesaw leptogenesis: the heavy-neutrino sector normally contains enough freedom that low-energy neutrino observables provide little information about baryogenesis. 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Neutrinos BSM **Xiao-Gang He, Zhong-Lv Huang, Raymond R. Volkas, Yu-Qi Xiao**  \n[arXiv:2609.12542](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12542)\n\nThis attacks the usual problem with type-I seesaw leptogenesis: the heavy-neutrino sector normally contains enough freedom that low-energy neutrino observables provide little information about baryogenesis.\n\nThey instead impose three predictive relations,\n\n\\[\nM_D=M_u,\\qquad M_D=M_d,\\qquad \\text{or}\\qquad M_D=M_\\ell,\n\\]\n\nso the seesaw equation allows the heavy Majorana matrix to be reconstructed from low-energy parameters.\n\nThey evolve the flavored asymmetries using **density-matrix Boltzmann equations**. Viable thermal leptogenesis occurs for normal ordering with nonzero Majorana phases in the down-quark-like and charged-lepton-like constructions. Successful points favor a close heavy-neutrino pair,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|M_i-M_j|}{M_i}<10^{-3},\n\\]\n\nalthough the authors stress that it is not the conventional resonant-leptogenesis limit.\n\nThe interesting experimental payoff is \\(0\\nu\\beta\\beta\\): some successful leptogenesis regions predict\n\n\\[\nm_{\\beta\\beta}\n\\]\n\nwithin reach of future **sub-10-meV** experiments such as LEGEND-1000, nEXO and CUPID-1T.\n\n**Significance:** this is exactly the kind of seesaw construction where cosmological baryogenesis and measurable low-energy neutrino observables cease to be almost completely disconnected.\n\n**Assessment:** **good neutrino-pheno paper; model-dependent but predictive.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-12775", "arxiv": "2609.12775", "title": "Constraints on Galactic Dark Photons from a DALI Prototype", "authors": "Javier De Miguel et al.", "tags": ["Dark matter"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "A straightforward but clean experimental result.", "section_text": "**Javier De Miguel et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.12775](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12775)\n\nA straightforward but clean experimental result.\n\nThe DALI haloscope prototype analyzed **36 hours** of data around\n\n\\[\n\\nu\\simeq6.9~{\\rm GHz},\n\\]\n\ncorresponding to a dark-photon mass near\n\n\\[\nm_{A'}=28.54~\\mu{\\rm eV}.\n\\]\n\nNo statistically significant excess is observed, giving approximately\n\n\\[\n\\chi\\lesssim6.8\\times10^{-14},\n\\]\n\nwhich the authors report as the strongest laboratory exclusion in that narrow frequency range.\n\n**Significance:** not transformative, but a real experimental improvement in ultralight-vector DM parameter space rather than another forecast paper.\n\n**Assessment:** **incremental but solid experimental result.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12775", "editionDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Constraints on Galactic Dark Photons from a DALI Prototype Javier De Miguel et al. 2609.12775 A straightforward but clean experimental result. 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Dark matter **Javier De Miguel et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.12775](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12775)\n\nA straightforward but clean experimental result.\n\nThe DALI haloscope prototype analyzed **36 hours** of data around\n\n\\[\n\\nu\\simeq6.9~{\\rm GHz},\n\\]\n\ncorresponding to a dark-photon mass near\n\n\\[\nm_{A'}=28.54~\\mu{\\rm eV}.\n\\]\n\nNo statistically significant excess is observed, giving approximately\n\n\\[\n\\chi\\lesssim6.8\\times10^{-14},\n\\]\n\nwhich the authors report as the strongest laboratory exclusion in that narrow frequency range.\n\n**Significance:** not transformative, but a real experimental improvement in ultralight-vector DM parameter space rather than another forecast paper.\n\n**Assessment:** **incremental but solid experimental result.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-12120", "arxiv": "2609.12120", "title": "Probing Lorentz Invariance Violation in Cosmogenic Neutrino Propagation with KM3-230213A", "authors": "Rodrigo Sasse, Rodrigo Guedes Lang, Rita de Cássia dos Anjos", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This one sits almost exactly at the intersection of UHECR propagation and UHE neutrino phenomenology.", "section_text": "**Rodrigo Sasse, Rodrigo Guedes Lang, Rita de Cássia dos Anjos**  \n[arXiv:2609.12120](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12120)\n\nThis one sits almost exactly at the intersection of UHECR propagation and UHE neutrino phenomenology.\n\nThey generate standard cosmogenic neutrino spectra using **CRPropa 3.2**, then introduce superluminal neutrino LIV producing neutrino splitting. The basic spectral effect is\n\n\\[\n{\\rm LIV}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{suppression at the highest energies},\\\\\n\\text{pile-up/enhancement at PeV--EeV energies}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nUsing KM3-230213A as a benchmark, they find sensitivity to dimensionful LIV coefficients roughly in the range\n\n\\[\n10^{-24}\\lesssim\n\\delta_{\\nu,1}/{\\rm eV^{-1}}\n\\lesssim10^{-22}.\n\\]\n\nLarger values can generate too many neutrinos in energy intervals already constrained by IceCube and Auger.\n\nThe important caveat is explicitly acknowledged by the authors: **one KM3NeT event does not permit a formal LIV constraint**, and the inferred sensitivity depends strongly on the assumed UHECR source population.\n\n**Significance:** the study connects UHECR propagation to cosmogenic-neutrino phenomenology, but its physical conclusion remains exploratory rather than a formal bound.\n\n**Assessment:** **interesting/speculative phenomenology.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/14/index.html#paper-2609-12120", "editionDate": "14 September 2026", "searchText": "Probing Lorentz Invariance Violation in Cosmogenic Neutrino Propagation with KM3-230213A Rodrigo Sasse, Rodrigo Guedes Lang, Rita de Cássia dos Anjos 2609.12120 This one sits almost exactly at the intersection of UHECR propagation and UHE neutrino phenomenology. 2026-09-14 14 September 2026 Neutrinos BSM **Rodrigo Sasse, Rodrigo Guedes Lang, Rita de Cássia dos Anjos**  \n[arXiv:2609.12120](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.12120)\n\nThis one sits almost exactly at the intersection of UHECR propagation and UHE neutrino phenomenology.\n\nThey generate standard cosmogenic neutrino spectra using **CRPropa 3.2**, then introduce superluminal neutrino LIV producing neutrino splitting. The basic spectral effect is\n\n\\[\n{\\rm LIV}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{suppression at the highest energies},\\\\\n\\text{pile-up/enhancement at PeV--EeV energies}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nUsing KM3-230213A as a benchmark, they find sensitivity to dimensionful LIV coefficients roughly in the range\n\n\\[\n10^{-24}\\lesssim\n\\delta_{\\nu,1}/{\\rm eV^{-1}}\n\\lesssim10^{-22}.\n\\]\n\nLarger values can generate too many neutrinos in energy intervals already constrained by IceCube and Auger.\n\nThe important caveat is explicitly acknowledged by the authors: **one KM3NeT event does not permit a formal LIV constraint**, and the inferred sensitivity depends strongly on the assumed UHECR source population.\n\n**Significance:** the study connects UHECR propagation to cosmogenic-neutrino phenomenology, but its physical conclusion remains exploratory rather than a formal bound.\n\n**Assessment:** **interesting/speculative phenomenology.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10668", "arxiv": "2609.10668", "title": "The Dark Dimension and Majorana Neutrinos", "authors": "Arturo de Giorgi, Dhruv Pasari", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Oscillations", "BSM"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The setup is the “Dark Dimension” scenario motivated by swampland arguments: an extra spatial dimension with characteristic size", "section_text": "**Arturo de Giorgi, Dhruv Pasari**  \n[arXiv:2609.10668](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10668)\n\nThe setup is the “Dark Dimension” scenario motivated by swampland arguments: an extra spatial dimension with characteristic size\n\n\\[\nR \\sim 0.1-10~\\mu{\\rm m},\n\\]\n\nwith Majorana fermions propagating in the bulk and masses in roughly the \\(1-10\\) keV regime. The authors explicitly diagonalize the resulting neutrino/Kaluza–Klein system and then confront it with three rather different observables:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Daya Bay oscillations},\\qquad\n\\text{KATRIN } \\beta\\text{-decay},\\qquad\n\\text{KamLAND-Zen } 0\\nu\\beta\\beta .\n\\]\n\nThe technically interesting point is the neutrinoless-double-beta-decay calculation. They derive a closed expression for the effective Majorana mass including the KK tower and find that the tower can **screen the \\(0\\nu\\beta\\beta\\) amplitude** once the characteristic nuclear momentum exceeds the compactification and Majorana scales. Thus adding a tower of Majorana states does *not* simply add positive contributions to \\(m_{\\beta\\beta}\\); destructive structure emerges from the complete spectrum.\n\nWithin their preferred Dark-Dimension parameter window, **KATRIN becomes the strongest terrestrial constraint**, pushing the relevant Yukawa coupling to approximately\n\n\\[\ny\\lesssim {\\cal O}(10^{-3}),\n\\]\n\nwhile oscillation experiments and \\(0\\nu\\beta\\beta\\) probe complementary regions of the Majorana-mass parameter space.\n\n**Significance:** it is an unusually clean example where oscillations, direct kinematic neutrino-mass measurements and \\(0\\nu\\beta\\beta\\) constrain the *same explicit BSM neutrino construction*. The KK screening of \\(0\\nu\\beta\\beta\\) is a distinctive feature of the calculation.\n\n**Classification:** **new neutrino/BSM phenomenology; high-interest theory paper.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/13/index.html#paper-2609-10668", "editionDate": "13 September 2026", "searchText": "The Dark Dimension and Majorana Neutrinos Arturo de Giorgi, Dhruv Pasari 2609.10668 The setup is the “Dark Dimension” scenario motivated by swampland arguments: an extra spatial dimension with characteristic size 2026-09-13 13 September 2026 Neutrinos Oscillations BSM **Arturo de Giorgi, Dhruv Pasari**  \n[arXiv:2609.10668](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10668)\n\nThe setup is the “Dark Dimension” scenario motivated by swampland arguments: an extra spatial dimension with characteristic size\n\n\\[\nR \\sim 0.1-10~\\mu{\\rm m},\n\\]\n\nwith Majorana fermions propagating in the bulk and masses in roughly the \\(1-10\\) keV regime. The authors explicitly diagonalize the resulting neutrino/Kaluza–Klein system and then confront it with three rather different observables:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Daya Bay oscillations},\\qquad\n\\text{KATRIN } \\beta\\text{-decay},\\qquad\n\\text{KamLAND-Zen } 0\\nu\\beta\\beta .\n\\]\n\nThe technically interesting point is the neutrinoless-double-beta-decay calculation. They derive a closed expression for the effective Majorana mass including the KK tower and find that the tower can **screen the \\(0\\nu\\beta\\beta\\) amplitude** once the characteristic nuclear momentum exceeds the compactification and Majorana scales. Thus adding a tower of Majorana states does *not* simply add positive contributions to \\(m_{\\beta\\beta}\\); destructive structure emerges from the complete spectrum.\n\nWithin their preferred Dark-Dimension parameter window, **KATRIN becomes the strongest terrestrial constraint**, pushing the relevant Yukawa coupling to approximately\n\n\\[\ny\\lesssim {\\cal O}(10^{-3}),\n\\]\n\nwhile oscillation experiments and \\(0\\nu\\beta\\beta\\) probe complementary regions of the Majorana-mass parameter space.\n\n**Significance:** it is an unusually clean example where oscillations, direct kinematic neutrino-mass measurements and \\(0\\nu\\beta\\beta\\) constrain the *same explicit BSM neutrino construction*. The KK screening of \\(0\\nu\\beta\\beta\\) is a distinctive feature of the calculation.\n\n**Classification:** **new neutrino/BSM phenomenology; high-interest theory paper.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10636", "arxiv": "2609.10636", "title": "Nonthermal Solar Stalling of an Inelastic Scalar Signal in Xenon", "authors": "XinXin Qi, Hao Sun", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This provides a counterpoint to the solar-neutrino/LZ paper covered in the previous report.", "section_text": "**XinXin Qi, Hao Sun**  \n[arXiv:2609.10636](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10636)\n\nThis provides a counterpoint to the solar-neutrino/LZ paper covered in the previous report.\n\nThe authors construct a concrete anomaly-free\n\n\\[\nU(1)_{B-3L_\\tau}\n\\]\n\nmodel containing an \\(840\\) GeV complex scalar DM particle undergoing an endothermic transition with splitting\n\n\\[\n\\delta\\simeq 320~{\\rm keV},\n\\]\n\nmediated by a \\(160\\) MeV vector. Their benchmark gives about \\(0.96\\) expected accepted events in their reconstruction of the high-energy LZ search, i.e. roughly the scale required to interpret the single \\(248\\) keV LZ recoil candidate.\n\nBut the interesting physics is what happens **after DM is captured by the Sun**.\n\nInstead of assuming rapid thermalization into a compact central DM distribution, they evolve the captured particles in energy, angular momentum and internal state, including thermal nuclear motion, finite scattering rates and excited-state decay. Because the scattering is endothermic, the particles eventually reach orbital configurations for which further up-scattering is kinematically forbidden.\n\nThey find a nonthermal population that effectively **stalls around**\n\n\\[\nr_{\\rm eff}\\simeq0.194-0.198\\,R_\\odot,\n\\]\n\nrather than thermalizing in the solar core. The resulting annihilation rate is only\n\n\\[\n\\Gamma_{\\rm ann}\\sim1.3\\times10^{15}\\ {\\rm s^{-1}},\n\\]\n\nwhich in their benchmark is more than three orders of magnitude below their IceCube-based neutrino proxy.\n\nSo the takeaway is subtle:\n\n> Solar-neutrino constraints on *electroweak* inelastic DM cannot automatically be carried over to every inelastic-DM realization.\n\nThis paper provides an explicit light-mediator scalar counterexample.\n\n**Significance:** the lesson is broader than the LZ anomaly. Whenever one maps DM capture to a neutrino signal, the usual capture–thermalization–annihilation equilibrium assumption can fail badly if the scattering has nontrivial kinematic thresholds.\n\n**Classification:** **new model-specific phenomenology; particularly valuable as a counterpoint to broad solar-neutrino exclusion arguments.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/13/index.html#paper-2609-10636", "editionDate": "13 September 2026", "searchText": "Nonthermal Solar Stalling of an Inelastic Scalar Signal in Xenon XinXin Qi, Hao Sun 2609.10636 This provides a counterpoint to the solar-neutrino/LZ paper covered in the previous report. 2026-09-13 13 September 2026 Multimessenger **XinXin Qi, Hao Sun**  \n[arXiv:2609.10636](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10636)\n\nThis provides a counterpoint to the solar-neutrino/LZ paper covered in the previous report.\n\nThe authors construct a concrete anomaly-free\n\n\\[\nU(1)_{B-3L_\\tau}\n\\]\n\nmodel containing an \\(840\\) GeV complex scalar DM particle undergoing an endothermic transition with splitting\n\n\\[\n\\delta\\simeq 320~{\\rm keV},\n\\]\n\nmediated by a \\(160\\) MeV vector. Their benchmark gives about \\(0.96\\) expected accepted events in their reconstruction of the high-energy LZ search, i.e. roughly the scale required to interpret the single \\(248\\) keV LZ recoil candidate.\n\nBut the interesting physics is what happens **after DM is captured by the Sun**.\n\nInstead of assuming rapid thermalization into a compact central DM distribution, they evolve the captured particles in energy, angular momentum and internal state, including thermal nuclear motion, finite scattering rates and excited-state decay. Because the scattering is endothermic, the particles eventually reach orbital configurations for which further up-scattering is kinematically forbidden.\n\nThey find a nonthermal population that effectively **stalls around**\n\n\\[\nr_{\\rm eff}\\simeq0.194-0.198\\,R_\\odot,\n\\]\n\nrather than thermalizing in the solar core. The resulting annihilation rate is only\n\n\\[\n\\Gamma_{\\rm ann}\\sim1.3\\times10^{15}\\ {\\rm s^{-1}},\n\\]\n\nwhich in their benchmark is more than three orders of magnitude below their IceCube-based neutrino proxy.\n\nSo the takeaway is subtle:\n\n> Solar-neutrino constraints on *electroweak* inelastic DM cannot automatically be carried over to every inelastic-DM realization.\n\nThis paper provides an explicit light-mediator scalar counterexample.\n\n**Significance:** the lesson is broader than the LZ anomaly. Whenever one maps DM capture to a neutrino signal, the usual capture–thermalization–annihilation equilibrium assumption can fail badly if the scattering has nontrivial kinematic thresholds.\n\n**Classification:** **new model-specific phenomenology; particularly valuable as a counterpoint to broad solar-neutrino exclusion arguments.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10694", "arxiv": "2609.10694", "title": "Strong Black Hole Natal Kicks in Magnetized Accretion-Powered Explosions", "authors": "Sean E. Li, Ore Gottlieb, Brian D. Metzger", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Using 3D general-relativistic MHD simulations extending to ~30 s after collapse, they show that rapidly rotating, strongly magnetized collapsars can give newly formed black holes natal kicks of", "section_text": "**Sean E. Li, Ore Gottlieb, Brian D. Metzger**  \n[arXiv:2609.10694](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10694)\n\nUsing **3D general-relativistic MHD simulations extending to \\(\\sim30\\) s after collapse**, they show that rapidly rotating, strongly magnetized collapsars can give newly formed black holes natal kicks of\n\n\\[\nv_{\\rm kick}\\sim10^2-10^3~{\\rm km\\,s^{-1}},\n\\]\n\nsubstantially larger than the weak BH kicks often assumed.\n\nThree effects contribute:\n\n\\[\n\\text{asymmetric electromagnetic outflow},\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{magnetic-flux eruptions},\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n\\text{gravitational pull from asymmetric ejecta}.\n\\]\n\nFaster-spinning BHs tend to receive larger and more spin-aligned kicks. Particularly strong asymmetries occur when the system undergoes a delayed transition into a **magnetically arrested disk (MAD)** state.\n\nThe neat astrophysical connection is that the strong coherent magnetic flux required to generate these kicks is also precisely the ingredient invoked for relativistic **GRB jets and jet-driven hypernovae**. Thus strong BH kicks might be an intrinsic consequence of some collapsar engines rather than an independent phenomenon.\n\nConsequences include BH retention in clusters, binary disruption, spin-orbit misalignment and formation channels of high-spin BHs in or near the pair-instability mass gap.\n\n**Significance:** this connects GRB-engine MHD directly to the compact-object populations measured by gravitational waves.\n\n**Classification:** **genuinely new numerical result; strong high-energy-astrophysics paper.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/13/index.html#paper-2609-10694", "editionDate": "13 September 2026", "searchText": "Strong Black Hole Natal Kicks in Magnetized Accretion-Powered Explosions Sean E. Li, Ore Gottlieb, Brian D. Metzger 2609.10694 Using 3D general-relativistic MHD simulations extending to ~30 s after collapse, they show that rapidly rotating, strongly magnetized collapsars can give newly formed black holes natal kicks of 2026-09-13 13 September 2026 Multimessenger **Sean E. Li, Ore Gottlieb, Brian D. Metzger**  \n[arXiv:2609.10694](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10694)\n\nUsing **3D general-relativistic MHD simulations extending to \\(\\sim30\\) s after collapse**, they show that rapidly rotating, strongly magnetized collapsars can give newly formed black holes natal kicks of\n\n\\[\nv_{\\rm kick}\\sim10^2-10^3~{\\rm km\\,s^{-1}},\n\\]\n\nsubstantially larger than the weak BH kicks often assumed.\n\nThree effects contribute:\n\n\\[\n\\text{asymmetric electromagnetic outflow},\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{magnetic-flux eruptions},\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n\\text{gravitational pull from asymmetric ejecta}.\n\\]\n\nFaster-spinning BHs tend to receive larger and more spin-aligned kicks. Particularly strong asymmetries occur when the system undergoes a delayed transition into a **magnetically arrested disk (MAD)** state.\n\nThe neat astrophysical connection is that the strong coherent magnetic flux required to generate these kicks is also precisely the ingredient invoked for relativistic **GRB jets and jet-driven hypernovae**. Thus strong BH kicks might be an intrinsic consequence of some collapsar engines rather than an independent phenomenon.\n\nConsequences include BH retention in clusters, binary disruption, spin-orbit misalignment and formation channels of high-spin BHs in or near the pair-instability mass gap.\n\n**Significance:** this connects GRB-engine MHD directly to the compact-object populations measured by gravitational waves.\n\n**Classification:** **genuinely new numerical result; strong high-energy-astrophysics paper.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10736", "arxiv": "2609.10736", "title": "Teglon: A Pixel-Level Pipeline for Galaxy-Informed Gravitational-Wave Follow-up Planning and Efficiency Analysis", "authors": "D. A. Coulter, P. Darc, C. D. Kilpatrick, R. J. Foley, P. McGill, S. D. Wyatt, R. Hausen", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This is not a physics discovery but potentially useful multimessenger infrastructure.", "section_text": "**D. A. Coulter, P. Darc, C. D. Kilpatrick, R. J. Foley, P. McGill, S. D. Wyatt, R. Hausen**  \n[arXiv:2609.10736](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10736)\n\nThis is not a physics discovery but potentially useful multimessenger infrastructure.\n\nTeglon takes a three-dimensional GW localization posterior and combines it with a **3D galaxy-completeness model**, producing a revised probability map informed by the known galaxy distribution. It can then ingest an arbitrary telescope footprint and continuously interpolate between pure galaxy targeting and ordinary sky tiling.\n\nThe clever extension is integration with **Redback**, allowing pixel-by-pixel detection efficiencies to be computed for transient models. The pipeline evaluates more than\n\n\\[\nP(\\mathrm{source\\ is\\ here}),\n\\]\n\nbut effectively\n\n\\[\nP(\\mathrm{detect}\\mid\\mathrm{source\\ here},\\,\n\\mathrm{instrument},\\,t,\\,\\mathrm{transient\\ model}).\n\\]\n\nThat is much more useful for assessing the actual efficiency of a follow-up campaign.\n\nThey report particularly substantial gains for telescopes with fields of view \\(\\lesssim1~{\\rm deg}^2\\), and Teglon has already been used through O3/O4 and is being prepared for O5.\n\n**Significance:** the tool quantitatively connects GW posterior volumes to gamma-ray, X-ray and optical counterpart searches.\n\n**Classification:** **methodological/tool paper, not a new astrophysical result.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/13/index.html#paper-2609-10736", "editionDate": "13 September 2026", "searchText": "Teglon: A Pixel-Level Pipeline for Galaxy-Informed Gravitational-Wave Follow-up Planning and Efficiency Analysis D. A. Coulter, P. Darc, C. D. Kilpatrick, R. J. Foley, P. McGill, S. D. Wyatt, R. Hausen 2609.10736 This is not a physics discovery but potentially useful multimessenger infrastructure. 2026-09-13 13 September 2026 Multimessenger **D. A. Coulter, P. Darc, C. D. Kilpatrick, R. J. Foley, P. McGill, S. D. Wyatt, R. Hausen**  \n[arXiv:2609.10736](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10736)\n\nThis is not a physics discovery but potentially useful multimessenger infrastructure.\n\nTeglon takes a three-dimensional GW localization posterior and combines it with a **3D galaxy-completeness model**, producing a revised probability map informed by the known galaxy distribution. It can then ingest an arbitrary telescope footprint and continuously interpolate between pure galaxy targeting and ordinary sky tiling.\n\nThe clever extension is integration with **Redback**, allowing pixel-by-pixel detection efficiencies to be computed for transient models. The pipeline evaluates more than\n\n\\[\nP(\\mathrm{source\\ is\\ here}),\n\\]\n\nbut effectively\n\n\\[\nP(\\mathrm{detect}\\mid\\mathrm{source\\ here},\\,\n\\mathrm{instrument},\\,t,\\,\\mathrm{transient\\ model}).\n\\]\n\nThat is much more useful for assessing the actual efficiency of a follow-up campaign.\n\nThey report particularly substantial gains for telescopes with fields of view \\(\\lesssim1~{\\rm deg}^2\\), and Teglon has already been used through O3/O4 and is being prepared for O5.\n\n**Significance:** the tool quantitatively connects GW posterior volumes to gamma-ray, X-ray and optical counterpart searches.\n\n**Classification:** **methodological/tool paper, not a new astrophysical result.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-11885", "arxiv": "2609.11885", "title": "Hierarchical Population Inference with Normalizing Flows for Binary Black Holes", "authors": "Leonardo Iampieri, Simone Mastrogiovanni", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "Instead of assuming a low-dimensional parametric BBH mass model, they represent the source population", "section_text": "**Leonardo Iampieri, Simone Mastrogiovanni**  \n[arXiv:2609.11885](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.11885)\n\nInstead of assuming a low-dimensional parametric BBH mass model, they represent the source population\n\n\\[\np(z,m_1,m_2)\n\\]\n\nwith a **normalizing flow embedded directly inside the hierarchical GW likelihood**. Event posterior samples propagate individual-event uncertainty, while injection campaigns encode detector selection effects.\n\nThere is a nice statistical subtlety here. Restricting the inference to the region sufficiently sampled by injections can itself create an apparent correlation between mass and redshift. They therefore introduce a mutual-information diagnostic comparing the inferred distribution with a redshift-independent reference subject to the *same support restriction*.\n\nApplied to **GWTC-5.0**, the reconstruction independently recovers population features near\n\n\\[\nm_1\\sim10\\,M_\\odot,\\qquad 35\\,M_\\odot,\n\\]\n\nbut finds **no significant evidence that the intrinsic primary-mass distribution evolves with redshift**.\n\n**Significance:** mainly methodology—the warning about spurious correlations induced by selection/support boundaries is applicable well beyond gravitational-wave inference.\n\n**Classification:** **strong methodological work; result itself is broadly consistent with existing LVK population conclusions.**\n\n---\n\nThere is therefore **no genuinely new Sunday arXiv result** in cosmic rays, neutrino astronomy, oscillations, DM or gamma-ray physics to report beyond Friday's batch. The five above are additional selections not covered in the previous report.", "path": "2026/09/13/index.html#paper-2609-11885", "editionDate": "13 September 2026", "searchText": "Hierarchical Population Inference with Normalizing Flows for Binary Black Holes Leonardo Iampieri, Simone Mastrogiovanni 2609.11885 Instead of assuming a low-dimensional parametric BBH mass model, they represent the source population 2026-09-13 13 September 2026 Multimessenger **Leonardo Iampieri, Simone Mastrogiovanni**  \n[arXiv:2609.11885](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.11885)\n\nInstead of assuming a low-dimensional parametric BBH mass model, they represent the source population\n\n\\[\np(z,m_1,m_2)\n\\]\n\nwith a **normalizing flow embedded directly inside the hierarchical GW likelihood**. Event posterior samples propagate individual-event uncertainty, while injection campaigns encode detector selection effects.\n\nThere is a nice statistical subtlety here. Restricting the inference to the region sufficiently sampled by injections can itself create an apparent correlation between mass and redshift. They therefore introduce a mutual-information diagnostic comparing the inferred distribution with a redshift-independent reference subject to the *same support restriction*.\n\nApplied to **GWTC-5.0**, the reconstruction independently recovers population features near\n\n\\[\nm_1\\sim10\\,M_\\odot,\\qquad 35\\,M_\\odot,\n\\]\n\nbut finds **no significant evidence that the intrinsic primary-mass distribution evolves with redshift**.\n\n**Significance:** mainly methodology—the warning about spurious correlations induced by selection/support boundaries is applicable well beyond gravitational-wave inference.\n\n**Classification:** **strong methodological work; result itself is broadly consistent with existing LVK population conclusions.**\n\n---\n\nThere is therefore **no genuinely new Sunday arXiv result** in cosmic rays, neutrino astronomy, oscillations, DM or gamma-ray physics to report beyond Friday's batch. The five above are additional selections not covered in the previous report."}, {"id": "paper-2609-10931", "arxiv": "2609.10931", "title": "IceCube neutrino point-source searches in the direction of the KM3NeT ultra-high-energy event", "authors": "IceCube Collaboration — R. Abbasi et al.", "tags": ["Neutrinos"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "IceCube uses 15 years of data to search the direction of KM3-230213A, the extraordinary KM3NeT event with reconstructed energy around 200 PeV, under three temporal hypotheses: a steady source, a flare coincident with the KM3NeT event,…", "section_text": "**IceCube Collaboration — R. Abbasi et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.10931](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10931)\n\nIceCube uses **15 years of data** to search the direction of KM3-230213A, the extraordinary KM3NeT event with reconstructed energy around \\(200\\) PeV, under three temporal hypotheses: a steady source, a flare coincident with the KM3NeT event, and a transient occurring at an arbitrary time. They find **no evidence for neutrino emission in any search** and derive point-source flux limits. The important conclusion is that if KM3-230213A came from an astrophysical point source, the simplest interpretation in which that source produces an \\(E^{-2}\\)-like flux at the normalization inferred from the single KM3NeT event is now **strongly constrained**, both for steady and transient scenarios.\n\n**Significance:** this significantly sharpens the astrophysical interpretation problem surrounding the KM3NeT event. The combination of one extraordinarily energetic KM3NeT neutrino and a long IceCube null exposure is becoming an interesting consistency test rather than merely an “extreme-energy neutrino” story.\n\n**Classification:** **genuinely new experimental constraint.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/12/index.html#paper-2609-10931", "editionDate": "12 September 2026", "searchText": "IceCube neutrino point-source searches in the direction of the KM3NeT ultra-high-energy event IceCube Collaboration — R. Abbasi et al. 2609.10931 IceCube uses 15 years of data to search the direction of KM3-230213A, the extraordinary KM3NeT event with reconstructed energy around 200 PeV, under three temporal hypotheses: a steady source, a flare coincident with the KM3NeT event,… 2026-09-12 12 September 2026 Neutrinos **IceCube Collaboration — R. Abbasi et al.**  \n[arXiv:2609.10931](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10931)\n\nIceCube uses **15 years of data** to search the direction of KM3-230213A, the extraordinary KM3NeT event with reconstructed energy around \\(200\\) PeV, under three temporal hypotheses: a steady source, a flare coincident with the KM3NeT event, and a transient occurring at an arbitrary time. They find **no evidence for neutrino emission in any search** and derive point-source flux limits. The important conclusion is that if KM3-230213A came from an astrophysical point source, the simplest interpretation in which that source produces an \\(E^{-2}\\)-like flux at the normalization inferred from the single KM3NeT event is now **strongly constrained**, both for steady and transient scenarios.\n\n**Significance:** this significantly sharpens the astrophysical interpretation problem surrounding the KM3NeT event. The combination of one extraordinarily energetic KM3NeT neutrino and a long IceCube null exposure is becoming an interesting consistency test rather than merely an “extreme-energy neutrino” story.\n\n**Classification:** **genuinely new experimental constraint.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10176", "arxiv": "2609.10176", "title": "Extended gamma-ray emission in the vicinity of Westerlund 1 and Kes 41 seen by Fermi-LAT", "authors": "L. Tibaldo, R. Bernet, L. Härer, M. Lemoine-Goumard, L. Mohrmann, G. Peron, B. Reville, T. Vieu", "tags": ["Gamma rays", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This paper appeared in the preceding announcement and is included in this first journal-club report. Using more than 16 years of Fermi-LAT data above 0.8 GeV, the authors identify three new extended, soft-spectrum gamma-ray components…", "section_text": "**L. Tibaldo, R. Bernet, L. Härer, M. Lemoine-Goumard, L. Mohrmann, G. Peron, B. Reville, T. Vieu**  \n[arXiv:2609.10176](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10176)\n\nThis paper appeared in the preceding announcement and is included in this first journal-club report. Using **more than 16 years of Fermi-LAT data above \\(0.8\\) GeV**, the authors identify **three new extended, soft-spectrum gamma-ray components** in the Westerlund 1/Kes 41 region. One is associated with the natal cloud of Kes 41 and is statistically preferred over the previously used point-source description. Two others overlap neutral gas within roughly \\(100\\) pc of the edge of the Westerlund 1 superbubble.\n\nThere is an important caveat: they cannot yet cleanly distinguish **real locally injected CR populations** from residual mismodelling of the Galactic interstellar emission. If the components are physical, the Kes 41 emission requires \\(\\lesssim5\\%\\) of the SNR energy budget in accelerated particles, while the two Wd1 components correspond to a gamma-ray luminosity of roughly \\(10^{-4}\\) of the cluster-wind mechanical power.\n\n**Significance:** this is precisely the sort of intermediate-scale CR-transport phenomenology that can alter how one interprets TeV–PeV emission and neutrino expectations from stellar-cluster environments.\n\n**Classification:** **new observational result; interpretation not yet unique.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/12/index.html#paper-2609-10176", "editionDate": "12 September 2026", "searchText": "Extended gamma-ray emission in the vicinity of Westerlund 1 and Kes 41 seen by Fermi-LAT L. Tibaldo, R. Bernet, L. Härer, M. Lemoine-Goumard, L. Mohrmann, G. Peron, B. Reville, T. Vieu 2609.10176 This paper appeared in the preceding announcement and is included in this first journal-club report. Using more than 16 years of Fermi-LAT data above 0.8 GeV, the authors identify three new extended, soft-spectrum gamma-ray components… 2026-09-12 12 September 2026 Gamma rays Multimessenger **L. Tibaldo, R. Bernet, L. Härer, M. Lemoine-Goumard, L. Mohrmann, G. Peron, B. Reville, T. Vieu**  \n[arXiv:2609.10176](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10176)\n\nThis paper appeared in the preceding announcement and is included in this first journal-club report. Using **more than 16 years of Fermi-LAT data above \\(0.8\\) GeV**, the authors identify **three new extended, soft-spectrum gamma-ray components** in the Westerlund 1/Kes 41 region. One is associated with the natal cloud of Kes 41 and is statistically preferred over the previously used point-source description. Two others overlap neutral gas within roughly \\(100\\) pc of the edge of the Westerlund 1 superbubble.\n\nThere is an important caveat: they cannot yet cleanly distinguish **real locally injected CR populations** from residual mismodelling of the Galactic interstellar emission. If the components are physical, the Kes 41 emission requires \\(\\lesssim5\\%\\) of the SNR energy budget in accelerated particles, while the two Wd1 components correspond to a gamma-ray luminosity of roughly \\(10^{-4}\\) of the cluster-wind mechanical power.\n\n**Significance:** this is precisely the sort of intermediate-scale CR-transport phenomenology that can alter how one interprets TeV–PeV emission and neutrino expectations from stellar-cluster environments.\n\n**Classification:** **new observational result; interpretation not yet unique.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-11833", "arxiv": "2609.11833", "title": "Solar Neutrino Constraints on Inelastic Dark Matter Scattering in Light of Recent LUX-ZEPLIN Observations", "authors": "Thong T. Q. Nguyen, Tim Linden, Dan Hooper", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter"], "label": "★★★ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The recent LZ high-recoil candidate has encouraged DM models in which the recoil spectrum is strongly momentum-dependent or the scattering is inelastic, allowing a large high-energy recoil without producing the many low-energy events…", "section_text": "**Thong T. Q. Nguyen, Tim Linden, Dan Hooper**  \n[arXiv:2609.11833](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.11833)\n\nThe recent LZ high-recoil candidate has encouraged DM models in which the recoil spectrum is strongly momentum-dependent or the scattering is inelastic, allowing a large high-energy recoil without producing the many low-energy events expected from conventional elastic WIMPs. Nguyen, Linden and Hooper point out an independent astroparticle constraint: such particles can be captured in the Sun, annihilate there, and generate **high-energy solar neutrinos**. IceCube sees no corresponding excess.\n\nTheir conclusion is that IceCube solar-neutrino observations **strongly constrain a substantial part of the inelastic-DM parameter space relevant to the LZ event**. This tests the recoil interpretation through an independent messenger.\n\nFor perspective, LZ itself reports only **2.6σ local significance**, approximately a \\(0.5\\%\\) background probability, and explicitly does **not** claim a DM discovery.\n\n**Significance:** a multimessenger connection between direct detection, solar capture and neutrino-telescope observations.\n\n**Classification:** **new phenomenological constraint on interpretations of the LZ candidate.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/12/index.html#paper-2609-11833", "editionDate": "12 September 2026", "searchText": "Solar Neutrino Constraints on Inelastic Dark Matter Scattering in Light of Recent LUX-ZEPLIN Observations Thong T. Q. Nguyen, Tim Linden, Dan Hooper 2609.11833 The recent LZ high-recoil candidate has encouraged DM models in which the recoil spectrum is strongly momentum-dependent or the scattering is inelastic, allowing a large high-energy recoil without producing the many low-energy events… 2026-09-12 12 September 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter **Thong T. Q. Nguyen, Tim Linden, Dan Hooper**  \n[arXiv:2609.11833](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.11833)\n\nThe recent LZ high-recoil candidate has encouraged DM models in which the recoil spectrum is strongly momentum-dependent or the scattering is inelastic, allowing a large high-energy recoil without producing the many low-energy events expected from conventional elastic WIMPs. Nguyen, Linden and Hooper point out an independent astroparticle constraint: such particles can be captured in the Sun, annihilate there, and generate **high-energy solar neutrinos**. IceCube sees no corresponding excess.\n\nTheir conclusion is that IceCube solar-neutrino observations **strongly constrain a substantial part of the inelastic-DM parameter space relevant to the LZ event**. This tests the recoil interpretation through an independent messenger.\n\nFor perspective, LZ itself reports only **2.6σ local significance**, approximately a \\(0.5\\%\\) background probability, and explicitly does **not** claim a DM discovery.\n\n**Significance:** a multimessenger connection between direct detection, solar capture and neutrino-telescope observations.\n\n**Classification:** **new phenomenological constraint on interpretations of the LZ candidate.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10784", "arxiv": "2609.10784", "title": "Low-Mass Magnetic Monopoles in the Galaxy: Simulations and Comparison with Ultra-High-Energy Cosmic-Ray Data", "authors": "Ala’a AL-Zetoun, Igor Ostrovskiy, Peiwen Mi, Daniele Perri", "tags": ["Cosmic rays", "BSM"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The authors implement Galactic propagation of low-mass magnetic monopoles in a custom CRPropa-based simulation with a realistic Galactic magnetic field. Because magnetic monopoles are accelerated along magnetic-field structures rather…", "section_text": "**Ala’a AL-Zetoun, Igor Ostrovskiy, Peiwen Mi, Daniele Perri**  \n[arXiv:2609.10784](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10784)\n\nThe authors implement Galactic propagation of low-mass magnetic monopoles in a custom **CRPropa-based simulation with a realistic Galactic magnetic field**. Because magnetic monopoles are accelerated *along* magnetic-field structures rather than deflected in the ordinary \\(q\\,\\mathbf v\\times\\mathbf B\\) sense, their trajectories develop what the authors call **Galactic magnetic funnels**, producing strongly anisotropic arrival directions. They then compare the predicted directional/energy distributions with Pierre Auger data.\n\nThe data generally favor **zero monopole contribution**, and the profile-likelihood analysis yields a \\(90\\%\\) C.L. integral flux limit below roughly\n\n\\[\n2.1\\times10^{-23}\\ {\\rm cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}\\,sr^{-1}}.\n\\]\n\n**Significance:** beyond monopole phenomenology, the study develops methods for modelling a highly anisotropic exotic charged population propagating through the GMF.\n\n**Classification:** **new simulation + Auger constraint; niche but technically interesting.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/12/index.html#paper-2609-10784", "editionDate": "12 September 2026", "searchText": "Low-Mass Magnetic Monopoles in the Galaxy: Simulations and Comparison with Ultra-High-Energy Cosmic-Ray Data Ala’a AL-Zetoun, Igor Ostrovskiy, Peiwen Mi, Daniele Perri 2609.10784 The authors implement Galactic propagation of low-mass magnetic monopoles in a custom CRPropa-based simulation with a realistic Galactic magnetic field. Because magnetic monopoles are accelerated along magnetic-field structures rather… 2026-09-12 12 September 2026 Cosmic rays BSM **Ala’a AL-Zetoun, Igor Ostrovskiy, Peiwen Mi, Daniele Perri**  \n[arXiv:2609.10784](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10784)\n\nThe authors implement Galactic propagation of low-mass magnetic monopoles in a custom **CRPropa-based simulation with a realistic Galactic magnetic field**. Because magnetic monopoles are accelerated *along* magnetic-field structures rather than deflected in the ordinary \\(q\\,\\mathbf v\\times\\mathbf B\\) sense, their trajectories develop what the authors call **Galactic magnetic funnels**, producing strongly anisotropic arrival directions. They then compare the predicted directional/energy distributions with Pierre Auger data.\n\nThe data generally favor **zero monopole contribution**, and the profile-likelihood analysis yields a \\(90\\%\\) C.L. integral flux limit below roughly\n\n\\[\n2.1\\times10^{-23}\\ {\\rm cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}\\,sr^{-1}}.\n\\]\n\n**Significance:** beyond monopole phenomenology, the study develops methods for modelling a highly anisotropic exotic charged population propagating through the GMF.\n\n**Classification:** **new simulation + Auger constraint; niche but technically interesting.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10690", "arxiv": "2609.10690", "title": "EFT Approaches to Sommerfeld Enhancement and Bound States in Singular Potentials", "authors": "Arindam Bhattacharya, Rashmish K. Mishra, Tracy R. Slatyer", "tags": ["Multimessenger"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The issue is that singular long-range potentials make the usual Schrödinger boundary-condition treatment of Sommerfeld enhancement and bound-state formation ambiguous. They attack the problem both with velocity power counting in…", "section_text": "**Arindam Bhattacharya, Rashmish K. Mishra, Tracy R. Slatyer**  \n[arXiv:2609.10690](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10690)\n\nThe issue is that singular long-range potentials make the usual Schrödinger boundary-condition treatment of **Sommerfeld enhancement and bound-state formation** ambiguous. They attack the problem both with velocity power counting in nonrelativistic EFT and by explicitly regularizing the potential at short distance.\n\nFor a pseudoscalar mediator coupled to massive Dirac DM, an important result is that a **weakly coupled UV-complete theory need not generate Sommerfeld enhancement at all**. Conversely, derivative/higher-dimensional interactions can produce enhancement and bound states at weak coupling when there is a sufficiently large hierarchy between the EFT cutoff and \\(m_\\chi\\). In that case the enhancement retains genuine UV sensitivity.\n\n**Significance:** it is a useful warning against blindly inserting a singular potential into the Schrödinger equation and interpreting the resulting large enhancement as robust DM phenomenology.\n\n**Classification:** **conceptual/methodological advance rather than a new observational result.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/12/index.html#paper-2609-10690", "editionDate": "12 September 2026", "searchText": "EFT Approaches to Sommerfeld Enhancement and Bound States in Singular Potentials Arindam Bhattacharya, Rashmish K. Mishra, Tracy R. Slatyer 2609.10690 The issue is that singular long-range potentials make the usual Schrödinger boundary-condition treatment of Sommerfeld enhancement and bound-state formation ambiguous. They attack the problem both with velocity power counting in… 2026-09-12 12 September 2026 Multimessenger **Arindam Bhattacharya, Rashmish K. Mishra, Tracy R. Slatyer**  \n[arXiv:2609.10690](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10690)\n\nThe issue is that singular long-range potentials make the usual Schrödinger boundary-condition treatment of **Sommerfeld enhancement and bound-state formation** ambiguous. They attack the problem both with velocity power counting in nonrelativistic EFT and by explicitly regularizing the potential at short distance.\n\nFor a pseudoscalar mediator coupled to massive Dirac DM, an important result is that a **weakly coupled UV-complete theory need not generate Sommerfeld enhancement at all**. Conversely, derivative/higher-dimensional interactions can produce enhancement and bound states at weak coupling when there is a sufficiently large hierarchy between the EFT cutoff and \\(m_\\chi\\). In that case the enhancement retains genuine UV sensitivity.\n\n**Significance:** it is a useful warning against blindly inserting a singular potential into the Schrödinger equation and interpreting the resulting large enhancement as robust DM phenomenology.\n\n**Classification:** **conceptual/methodological advance rather than a new observational result.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10674", "arxiv": "2609.10674", "title": "Re-examining the sensitivity of JWST to decaying axion dark matter", "authors": "Caleb Gemmell, Christopher Dessert, Andrea Caputo, Joshua W. Foster", "tags": ["Dark matter"], "label": "★★☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "This is a useful corrective paper. Different JWST searches for the infrared line from a→γγ had been claiming coupling sensitivities differing by roughly an order of magnitude. The authors redo the data reduction and use flexible…", "section_text": "**Caleb Gemmell, Christopher Dessert, Andrea Caputo, Joshua W. Foster**  \n[arXiv:2609.10674](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10674)\n\nThis is a useful corrective paper. Different JWST searches for the infrared line from \\(a\\rightarrow\\gamma\\gamma\\) had been claiming coupling sensitivities differing by roughly **an order of magnitude**. The authors redo the data reduction and use flexible non-parametric inference, concluding that some of the strongest previously claimed sensitivities are simply not achievable with the datasets used.\n\nTheir robust analysis nevertheless excludes QCD-axion DM over approximately\n\n\\[\nm_a\\simeq 0.5-2.5\\ {\\rm eV},\n\\]\n\nand gives complementary ALP constraints at \\(0.1-0.5\\) eV. Importantly, they argue that the present analyses are approaching a **systematics floor**, so simply accumulating more exposure will not give the naive statistical improvement unless JWST instrumental modelling and reduction pipelines improve.\n\n**Significance:** not “new physics discovered,” but exactly the kind of systematic reassessment that determines whether a spectacular exclusion plot is actually trustworthy.\n\n**Classification:** **important re-analysis/systematics paper.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/12/index.html#paper-2609-10674", "editionDate": "12 September 2026", "searchText": "Re-examining the sensitivity of JWST to decaying axion dark matter Caleb Gemmell, Christopher Dessert, Andrea Caputo, Joshua W. Foster 2609.10674 This is a useful corrective paper. Different JWST searches for the infrared line from a→γγ had been claiming coupling sensitivities differing by roughly an order of magnitude. The authors redo the data reduction and use flexible… 2026-09-12 12 September 2026 Dark matter **Caleb Gemmell, Christopher Dessert, Andrea Caputo, Joshua W. Foster**  \n[arXiv:2609.10674](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10674)\n\nThis is a useful corrective paper. Different JWST searches for the infrared line from \\(a\\rightarrow\\gamma\\gamma\\) had been claiming coupling sensitivities differing by roughly **an order of magnitude**. The authors redo the data reduction and use flexible non-parametric inference, concluding that some of the strongest previously claimed sensitivities are simply not achievable with the datasets used.\n\nTheir robust analysis nevertheless excludes QCD-axion DM over approximately\n\n\\[\nm_a\\simeq 0.5-2.5\\ {\\rm eV},\n\\]\n\nand gives complementary ALP constraints at \\(0.1-0.5\\) eV. Importantly, they argue that the present analyses are approaching a **systematics floor**, so simply accumulating more exposure will not give the naive statistical improvement unless JWST instrumental modelling and reduction pipelines improve.\n\n**Significance:** not “new physics discovered,” but exactly the kind of systematic reassessment that determines whether a spectacular exclusion plot is actually trustworthy.\n\n**Classification:** **important re-analysis/systematics paper.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-10671", "arxiv": "2609.10671", "title": "Ultralight Axial Dark Matter", "authors": "Anson Hook, Junwu Huang, Kevin Zhou", "tags": ["Dark matter"], "label": "★☆☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "They explore ultralight DM as an axial vector field rather than the usual scalar/axion or vector dark photon. Fermionic couplings generate spin torques that can be parametrically enhanced relative to axion DM. They also construct an…", "section_text": "**Anson Hook, Junwu Huang, Kevin Zhou**  \n[arXiv:2609.10671](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10671)\n\nThey explore ultralight DM as an **axial vector field** rather than the usual scalar/axion or vector dark photon. Fermionic couplings generate spin torques that can be parametrically enhanced relative to axion DM. They also construct an analogue of the axion-photon coupling, though its consistent realization requires a photon mass, and discuss signatures involving polarimetry, superconducting cavities, electric fields and the CMB. The massive axial vector description is dual to a massive two-form/Kalb–Ramond field.\n\n**Significance:** an expansion of the ultralight-DM model space with unusual experimental signatures.\n\n**Classification:** **new theoretical framework/model-building.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/12/index.html#paper-2609-10671", "editionDate": "12 September 2026", "searchText": "Ultralight Axial Dark Matter Anson Hook, Junwu Huang, Kevin Zhou 2609.10671 They explore ultralight DM as an axial vector field rather than the usual scalar/axion or vector dark photon. Fermionic couplings generate spin torques that can be parametrically enhanced relative to axion DM. They also construct an… 2026-09-12 12 September 2026 Dark matter **Anson Hook, Junwu Huang, Kevin Zhou**  \n[arXiv:2609.10671](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10671)\n\nThey explore ultralight DM as an **axial vector field** rather than the usual scalar/axion or vector dark photon. Fermionic couplings generate spin torques that can be parametrically enhanced relative to axion DM. They also construct an analogue of the axion-photon coupling, though its consistent realization requires a photon mass, and discuss signatures involving polarimetry, superconducting cavities, electric fields and the CMB. The massive axial vector description is dual to a massive two-form/Kalb–Ramond field.\n\n**Significance:** an expansion of the ultralight-DM model space with unusual experimental signatures.\n\n**Classification:** **new theoretical framework/model-building.**\n\n---"}, {"id": "paper-2609-11511", "arxiv": "2609.11511", "title": "Cosmological Evolution of Primordial Black Holes: UV/IR Decoupling and the KM3NeT 220 PeV Neutrino Anomaly", "authors": "Debdatta Dey, Tausif Parvez, S. Shankaranarayanan", "tags": ["Neutrinos", "Dark matter", "Multimessenger"], "label": "★☆☆ · arXiv highlight", "teaser": "The proposal is that KM3-230213A came from the terminal Hawking evaporation of a primordial black hole. The authors embed the PBH in a McVittie cosmological spacetime: early-time cosmological accretion and expansion modify the PBH…", "section_text": "**Debdatta Dey, Tausif Parvez, S. Shankaranarayanan**  \n[arXiv:2609.11511](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.11511)\n\nThe proposal is that KM3-230213A came from the **terminal Hawking evaporation of a primordial black hole**. The authors embed the PBH in a McVittie cosmological spacetime: early-time cosmological accretion and expansion modify the PBH lifetime and initial mass required to explode today, while the final \\(z\\simeq0\\) phase becomes essentially Schwarzschild. They argue that this can produce a large local \\(\\sim220\\) PeV neutrino signal while avoiding excessive diffuse early-time emission.\n\nThis theoretical interpretation is **highly model-dependent**, and today's IceCube point-source paper further constrains source interpretations of KM3-230213A.\n\n**Significance:** the paper explores an exotic origin for the KM3NeT event, with viability dependent on the underlying PBH and source assumptions.\n\n**Classification:** **speculative interpretation.**\n\n---", "path": "2026/09/12/index.html#paper-2609-11511", "editionDate": "12 September 2026", "searchText": "Cosmological Evolution of Primordial Black Holes: UV/IR Decoupling and the KM3NeT 220 PeV Neutrino Anomaly Debdatta Dey, Tausif Parvez, S. Shankaranarayanan 2609.11511 The proposal is that KM3-230213A came from the terminal Hawking evaporation of a primordial black hole. The authors embed the PBH in a McVittie cosmological spacetime: early-time cosmological accretion and expansion modify the PBH… 2026-09-12 12 September 2026 Neutrinos Dark matter Multimessenger **Debdatta Dey, Tausif Parvez, S. Shankaranarayanan**  \n[arXiv:2609.11511](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.11511)\n\nThe proposal is that KM3-230213A came from the **terminal Hawking evaporation of a primordial black hole**. The authors embed the PBH in a McVittie cosmological spacetime: early-time cosmological accretion and expansion modify the PBH lifetime and initial mass required to explode today, while the final \\(z\\simeq0\\) phase becomes essentially Schwarzschild. They argue that this can produce a large local \\(\\sim220\\) PeV neutrino signal while avoiding excessive diffuse early-time emission.\n\nThis theoretical interpretation is **highly model-dependent**, and today's IceCube point-source paper further constrains source interpretations of KM3-230213A.\n\n**Significance:** the paper explores an exotic origin for the KM3NeT event, with viability dependent on the underlying PBH and source assumptions.\n\n**Classification:** **speculative interpretation.**\n\n---"}];
